import type { GuideEntry } from "./types";

export const GUIDE_INDEX: readonly GuideEntry[] = [
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"What CASS is for","anchor":"card-what-cass-is-for","card":"","text":"CASS stands for the Catastrophe Analytics and Scenario Suite. It estimates how much money a group of insured buildings could lose if earthquakes strike them. Large earthquakes are rare. Any one insurer may go decades without a serious one and then face a very large loss in a single day. Because history alone does not contain enough large earthquakes to show what could happen, a catastrophe model imagines thousands of earthquakes that are scientifically plausible, works out the damage each one would do to your buildings, and adds up what that damage would cost. That gives a picture of both the typical year and the rare, very bad year. CASS does that calculation for a portfolio you give it, and shows you where the losses come from. CASS is a research tool Its results help people understand earthquake risk and test ideas. They are not a basis for setting prices or reserves, even when a reviewer has approved them."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"Signing in","anchor":"card-signing-in","card":"","text":"CASS runs in a browser, on an address someone in your organisation will give you. Your administrator creates the account and sets its role, which decides what you can see; there is no way to sign yourself up. On the sign-in screen, type your Username (not an email address) and Password, then press Sign in. The eye button beside the password shows what you have typed, which is worth using if a sign-in fails. If it still fails, the screen says why; a password nobody can remember is an administrator's job to reset. You stay signed in until you press Sign out at the bottom of the sidebar, or until the platform ends your session. Work already running carries on whether or not you stay signed in."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"How to use this guide","anchor":"card-how-to-use-this-guide","card":"","text":"If you are new, read this page first, then the Glossary, then the sections in order. Each one builds on the one before. If you are looking something up, jump to the section you need using the tabs above. You can copy the address from your browser to send someone straight to a section. Numbered steps are things to do, in order. The grey label under a step says where on screen to do it. An arrow means “then”: Models → Build means click Models in the sidebar on the left, then the Build tab at the top of that page. Words in bold are usually the exact text of a button, field or heading on the screen, so you can look for them. Shaded example boxes use made-up numbers to show how an idea works. They are not real results. “For scripting (API)” lines are for people who drive CASS from code instead of the screens. You can ignore them."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"How CASS calculates a loss, in plain words","anchor":"card-how-cass-calculates-a-loss-in-plain-words","card":"","text":"It helps to know the whole calculation before learning the screens, because every screen prepares one part of it. This is the full chain, in the order it happens. Divide the country into small squares. Calculating the shaking at every single address would take far too long, so the country is covered with a grid of small squares called cells. Each cell is treated as one place. Imagine many years of earthquakes. A hazard model produced by earthquake scientists is used to invent many years of plausible earthquakes: 1,000 of them with the settings CASS starts from. Each imagined earthquake is called an event. Most years have only small ones; a few years have a very large one. Work out how hard each event shakes each cell. The shaking in each cell for each event is stored. This record is called the footprint. Put each of your properties in its cell. CASS reads the latitude and longitude of every property and finds which cell it falls in. Turn shaking into damage. A vulnerability function says how badly a particular type of building is damaged by a given strength of shaking, as a percentage of what it would cost to rebuild. An old brick house is damaged much more than a modern building designed for earthquakes. Turn damage into money. The damage percentage multiplied by the building's value is the ground-up loss: the full cost of repair. Apply the insurance. The policy's deductible (the part the policyholder pays first) and limit (the most the policy will pay) are applied, giving the insured loss. Then any reinsurance treaties are applied, giving the loss the insurer keeps: the loss net of reinsurance. Add it all up, year by year. The losses from every event in each imagined year are added together. With 1,000 years of totals, CASS can say what an average year costs and how bad the worst years are. Example: one warehouse, one imagined earthquake (made-up numbers) A warehouse is insured for 1,000,000. Its coordinates place it in cell 4,512. In imagined year 317, an earthquake shakes cell 4,512 hard. The vulnerability function for this kind of warehouse says that level of shaking causes, on average, 12% damage. So the ground-up loss is 12% of 1,000,000, which is 120,000. (The engine also allows for the damage turning out higher or lower than the average.) The policy has a deductible of 20,000, which the owner pays first. So the insurer pays 100,000: the insured loss. The insurer has a 30% quota share treaty, so a reinsurer pays 30,000 of that. The insurer keeps 70,000: the loss net of reinsurance. CASS does this for every property, for every event, in every one of the 10,000 imagined years."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"What you need before a loss can be calculated","anchor":"card-what-you-need-before-a-loss-can-be-calculated","card":"","text":"The chain above needs four things to exist. The rest of this guide shows how each one is made. What What it is Who makes it, and where it is explained A grid The country divided into cells. A modeller. Grids, cells and tiles. A vulnerability set The vulnerability functions for every type of building in the country. A modeller. Build a model, part 3. A hazard set The imagined earthquakes and how hard each shakes every cell. A modeller. Build a model, part 5. A published portfolio Your list of properties, checked and then frozen so it cannot change. An analyst. Bring in a portfolio. The first three are joined together into a model version . A run takes one published portfolio and one model version and calculates the losses."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"The three losses a run can report","anchor":"card-the-three-losses-a-run-can-report","card":"","text":"The same earthquake damage can be counted in three ways, depending on whose money you are looking at. Each way is called a perspective. You choose one when you set up a run. Perspective What it measures What your portfolio must include for it Ground-up The full cost of repairing the damage, as if there were no insurance at all. The properties, where they are, and their values. Insured The part the insurance policies pay, after the policyholder has paid their deductible and after any policy limit is reached. The policies, with their deductibles and limits. Loss net of reinsurance The part the insurer is left paying itself, after its own insurers (reinsurers) have paid their share. The policies, and the reinsurance contracts. The amount the reinsurers pay is called the ceded loss. CASS does not show it as a separate number, but you can work it out: it is the insured loss minus the loss net of reinsurance. A note if you have used other tools: the loss engine inside CASS labels the third perspective ri (short for reinsurance), which makes it look like the amount paid by reinsurers. It is actually the amount the insurer keeps. CASS calls it “loss net of reinsurance” to avoid that confusion. A real run on this installation A portfolio of 64 properties in Jakarta and Bandung, with three reinsurance treaties, was run for all three perspectives. It took 96 seconds. The average yearly losses were: ground-up 566,728; insured 305,479; net of reinsurance 151,314. So the reinsurers paid 305,479 − 151,314 = 154,165. Those are real figures from this installation, but they are not exact truth: the same book calculated directly in OpenQuake came to 626,732 ground-up, about 10% more. Run and read results explains what that means for how much weight a number can carry."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"The journey: two tracks that meet at a run","anchor":"card-the-journey-two-tracks-that-meet-at-a-run","card":"","text":"CASS builds two things separately and joins them at the very end. Knowing which one you are on saves a lot of confusion later. The model is the science for a whole country: where earthquakes happen, how hard the ground shakes at each place, and how buildings respond. A modeller builds it once. It belongs to no project, and every project in the installation uses the same one. Your work is a project, the portfolio of properties inside it, and the results that come out. All of that belongs to the project, and only people who are members of it can see any of it. The two meet at the moment you start a run, which takes one published portfolio and one model version and calculates the losses. That is the only point at which they touch. The model for Indonesia may have been built months before your project existed; it sits outside your project, it is not yours, and deleting your project does not remove it. Which track you start on depends on your role. A modeller may work on the first one. Everybody else starts on the second, at Create or choose a project: for you that is step one, and nothing else you do on the platform is possible until it is done."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"Track 1: the model, built once for a country","anchor":"card-track-1-the-model-built-once-for-a-country","card":"","text":"Most people never do either of these steps, and neither of them needs a project. Both are usually finished long before a new analyst signs in for the first time, so read this to know what has already happened rather than as something to do."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Set up the platform","anchor":"step-track-1-the-model-built-once-for-a-country--set-up-the-platform","card":"Track 1: the model, built once for a country","step":1,"where":"An administrator, once for each installation","text":"An administrator, once for each installation Someone installs CASS, starts it, and tells it where to find the earthquake building data it needs. Most people never do this: CASS is already running when they first sign in. The instructions are in the file platform/docs/running-cass.md."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Build a model version","anchor":"step-track-1-the-model-built-once-for-a-country--build-a-model-version","card":"Track 1: the model, built once for a country","step":2,"where":"Models → Build and Models → Hazard (modellers only)","text":"Models → Build and Models → Hazard (modellers only) A modeller builds the grid, the vulnerability set and the hazard set for a country, and joins them into a model version. Build a model walks through every step. None of it is filed under a project. A model version is an asset of the whole installation, which is why a colleague's project can use the one you built, and why it survives when a project is deleted."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"Track 2: your work, inside a project","anchor":"card-track-2-your-work-inside-a-project","card":"","text":""},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Create or choose a project","anchor":"step-track-2-your-work-inside-a-project--create-or-choose-a-project","card":"Track 2: your work, inside a project","step":1,"where":"Portfolio dashboard","text":"Portfolio dashboard A project is the folder that owns everything that follows: the portfolios, the runs and the results. Nothing can be brought in or run until one is chosen, which is why screens ask for a project before they show you anything. On the Portfolio dashboard, click a project's name to work in it, or press New project to make one. Anyone can create a project, whatever their role, and whoever creates it owns it. A new project needs a name, a short reference (used to label its files, so keep it short and do not change it later) and a purpose. On a new installation there are no projects at all, so this is the first thing to do."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Bring in a portfolio","anchor":"step-track-2-your-work-inside-a-project--bring-in-a-portfolio","card":"Track 2: your work, inside a project","step":2,"where":"Exposure","text":"Exposure An analyst uploads the list of properties, checks and corrects how CASS has read it, adds the insurance and reinsurance details, and then publishes it so it can be used. Bring in a portfolio."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Run it","anchor":"step-track-2-your-work-inside-a-project--run-it","card":"Track 2: your work, inside a project","step":3,"where":"Analysis builder, then Run monitor","text":"Analysis builder, then Run monitor The analyst chooses the portfolio, the model version and the perspective, starts the run, and watches it progress. Run and read results. This is where the two tracks meet: your portfolio, from this project, and a model version from the shared catalogue. The run itself belongs to the project, and so do the results it produces."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"step","label":"Read the answer","anchor":"step-track-2-your-work-inside-a-project--read-the-answer","card":"Track 2: your work, inside a project","step":4,"where":"Results","text":"Results The results show the average yearly loss, how large the rare bad years are, which earthquakes and which places caused the most loss, and how one run compares with another."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","anchor":"card-the-portfolio-dashboard-where-you-land","card":"","text":"Portfolio dashboard is the first screen after signing in. It answers two questions: what is waiting for you, and which project are you working in."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"The five tiles across the top","anchor":"term-the-five-tiles-across-the-top","card":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","text":"Projects you can see, Runs in progress, Awaiting approval (work sitting at a gate), Imports to review (spreadsheets read but not yet turned into portfolios) and Portfolios to resolve (portfolios with problems that stop them being published). A tile showing 0 means nothing is waiting."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Coloured messages","anchor":"term-coloured-messages","card":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","text":"Under the tiles, CASS raises anything that needs attention: runs that failed, with a link to each; imports waiting for a review; and a warning listing model versions that are research prototypes, whose results must not be used for decisions."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Projects","anchor":"term-projects","card":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","text":"Every project you can see, with your role in it, how many portfolios it holds and how many runs are going. Click a project's name to work in it; that is what fills in the project shown in the header. New project creates one."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Recent runs","anchor":"term-recent-runs","card":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","text":"The last ten runs, newest first, with their state. Click one to open it in the Run monitor."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Portfolios with blocking findings","anchor":"term-portfolios-with-blocking-findings","card":"The Portfolio dashboard: where you land","text":"Portfolios that cannot be published until something in their data is fixed, with a link straight to the problem."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"What you can do depends on your role","anchor":"card-what-you-can-do-depends-on-your-role","card":"","text":"Every account is given one role. Your role decides which screens and buttons you see, so if a step in this guide mentions a button you cannot find, your role is the first thing to check. Your role is shown under your name at the bottom of the sidebar. Two things are the same for everybody, whatever their role. Anyone can create a project, and whoever creates one owns it. And a role is not access: it says what you may do in a project, while which projects you can see at all is decided separately, by being made a member of them. Role What it lets you do Portfolio analyst Bring in portfolios, run analyses and read results. Most day-to-day work happens with this role. Underwriter The same screens as a portfolio analyst. Catastrophe modeller Everything an analyst can do, and also build models: the Hazard and Build tabs under Models, where grids, vulnerability sets, hazard runs and model versions are made. Reviewer Check other people's work and approve or reject it at the points where CASS requires a second person's agreement (called gates), and approve results. Nobody, in any role, can approve their own request. A reviewer can also create a project and do an analyst's work inside it; approving is what the role adds, not all it allows. Platform administrator Everything the other roles can do, and also manage user accounts, the calculation engines, storage, and the record of who did what."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"Finding your way around the screen","anchor":"card-finding-your-way-around-the-screen","card":"","text":"Every screen has the same frame around it, so you always know what you are working on."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"The sidebar (left)","anchor":"term-the-sidebar-left","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"The main areas of CASS, listed in the order work usually happens: Portfolio dashboard, Exposure, Analysis builder, Run monitor, Results, Models, Administration. At the bottom are your name, your role and the Sign out button. On a narrow screen the sidebar shrinks to icons only; hold the pointer over an icon to see what that area is for."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"The header (top)","anchor":"term-the-header-top","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"On the left, the project you are working in. On the right, the link to this User guide."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"The bar under the header","anchor":"term-the-bar-under-the-header","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"Four items that tell you what the numbers on the screen belong to. Each is chosen on a particular screen and then remembered, even if you reload the page or come back tomorrow."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Project (in the header)","anchor":"term-project-in-the-header","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"A project is a folder for one piece of work: it holds the portfolios, runs and results that belong together. You must choose a project before you can do anything else. On the Portfolio dashboard, click the name of a project to choose it, or press New project to make one. A new project needs a name, a short reference (used to label its files, so keep it short and do not change it later) and a purpose. Anyone can create one, whatever their role, and the person who creates it becomes its owner; if the list is empty, either nobody has made one yet or you have not been added to any."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Model version (in the bar)","anchor":"term-model-version-in-the-bar","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"The model the next run will use. You choose it in the Analysis builder or in the Models catalogue. Beside it is a badge: a green Approved badge means the model is finished and its results may be approved for decisions, and a yellow Research only badge means it is not, so its results cannot be. Today every model version built in CASS carries the yellow badge."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Portfolio (in the bar)","anchor":"term-portfolio-in-the-bar","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"The list of properties you are working on, and which version of it. You choose it in Exposure → Portfolios or in the Analysis builder. It shows Draft while it can still be changed and Published once it is frozen. Only a published portfolio can be run."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Perspective (in the bar)","anchor":"term-perspective-in-the-bar","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"Which of the three losses you are looking at: ground-up, insured, or net of reinsurance. You choose it in the Analysis builder."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"term","label":"Run state (in the bar)","anchor":"term-run-state-in-the-bar","card":"Finding your way around the screen","text":"Whether a calculation is running in this project right now, and which step it has reached."},
  {"section":"start","sectionLabel":"Start here","kind":"card","label":"One limit to know before you start","anchor":"card-one-limit-to-know-before-you-start","card":"","text":"CASS can only calculate a loss where earthquake shaking has been calculated Building a grid and a vulnerability set for a country takes a few minutes. Calculating the shaking (the hazard) for a whole country takes hours of computer time, so it is only done where it is needed. At the moment, shaking has been calculated for one area only: Jakarta and Bandung in Indonesia. That area is 962 cells out of the 52,831 cells that cover all of Indonesia. A property outside that area is marked outside the domain , meaning outside the ground the model covers. This is deliberate. CASS could have quietly given those properties a loss of zero, but that would make the total look smaller than it really is without anyone noticing. Instead it tells you they could not be modelled. In practice: a portfolio in Jakarta and Bandung can be run from start to finish today. A portfolio spread across Indonesia needs the shaking calculated for the whole country first. A portfolio in another country needs a hazard model for that country."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"How this glossary is organised","anchor":"card-how-this-glossary-is-organised","card":"","text":"The terms are grouped by where you will meet them: first the ideas behind any catastrophe model, then the terms used when building a grid, the hazard and the vulnerability, then the terms for portfolios and insurance, and last the terms used when running a calculation and reading its results. Use your browser's find command (Ctrl+F, or Cmd+F on a Mac) to jump to a term."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"The basic ideas","anchor":"card-the-basic-ideas","card":"","text":"What every catastrophe model is built on."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Catastrophe model","anchor":"term-catastrophe-model","card":"The basic ideas","text":"A calculation that estimates the losses disasters could cause, including disasters that have not happened yet. It works by imagining a very large number of possible disasters and working out the damage from each. Every catastrophe model has three parts: the hazard (how strong the disaster is at each place), the vulnerability (how much damage that does), and the financial terms (who pays for the damage)."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Hazard","anchor":"term-hazard","card":"The basic ideas","text":"How strong a natural event is at a particular place. For an earthquake, that means how hard the ground shakes at that spot. The same earthquake shakes some places much harder than others: places closer to where it starts usually shake harder, and so do places on soft ground."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Vulnerability","anchor":"term-vulnerability","card":"The basic ideas","text":"How easily a building is damaged by a given strength of shaking. It is measured as the cost of the damage as a share of what it would cost to rebuild the building completely. Two buildings next to each other can have very different vulnerability: an old building of unreinforced brick might be badly damaged by shaking that a modern building designed for earthquakes survives with little harm."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Exposure","anchor":"term-exposure","card":"The basic ideas","text":"The things that could be damaged, and what they are worth. In CASS that means the insured properties: where each one is, what it is built from, what it is used for, how tall it is, and its value. Your portfolio is your exposure."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Peril and sub-peril","anchor":"term-peril-and-sub-peril","card":"The basic ideas","text":"A peril is a type of disaster, such as earthquake, flood or windstorm. A sub-peril is one particular way that disaster causes damage. An earthquake can damage buildings by shaking them, and also by causing a tsunami, a fire, a landslide, or by turning wet ground soft (liquefaction). CASS models the shaking only. Damage from the other sub-perils is not included in its numbers."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Event","anchor":"term-event","card":"The basic ideas","text":"One imagined earthquake: where it happens, how large it is, and how hard it shakes each place. Events are not earthquakes that really happened. They are earthquakes that could plausibly happen, invented by the hazard model based on what scientists know about the faults in the area."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Simulated years","anchor":"term-simulated-years","card":"The basic ideas","text":"The number of imagined years of earthquakes the model creates: 10,000 by default in CASS. Large earthquakes are so rare that even a hundred years of real history would include only a few. A 1-in-1,000-year loss is read from the worst imagined years, so it needs many of them: from 1,000 imagined years it is just the single worst year, and from 10,000 it is the tenth-worst, which is much steadier. Losses are also averaged across all the imagined years to give yearly figures. More imagined years take longer to calculate and more space to store."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"GEM","anchor":"term-gem","card":"The basic ideas","text":"The Global Earthquake Model Foundation, a not-for-profit organisation of earthquake scientists. It publishes earthquake hazard models, information about the kinds of buildings in each country, and vulnerability functions for those buildings. CASS uses GEM's published data, with GEM's written permission."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"OpenQuake","anchor":"term-openquake","card":"The basic ideas","text":"The earthquake calculation software made by GEM. CASS uses it to calculate the imagined earthquakes and the shaking they cause. You never use it directly; CASS runs it for you."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Oasis (Oasis LMF)","anchor":"term-oasis-oasis-lmf","card":"The basic ideas","text":"Free, open-source software used across the insurance industry to calculate losses. CASS gives it the shaking, the vulnerability functions and your portfolio, and Oasis calculates the losses. As with OpenQuake, CASS runs it for you."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"Grid terms","anchor":"card-grid-terms","card":"","text":"These are explained further, with a picture and worked numbers, in Grids, cells and tiles."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Latitude and longitude","anchor":"term-latitude-and-longitude","card":"Grid terms","text":"The two numbers that give any position on Earth. Latitude says how far north or south of the equator a place is: positive numbers are north, negative numbers are south. Longitude says how far east or west it is of Greenwich in London: positive numbers are east, negative numbers are west. For example, Jakarta is at about latitude −6.2 (6.2 degrees south) and longitude 106.8 (106.8 degrees east)."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Degree (°)","anchor":"term-degree","card":"Grid terms","text":"The unit latitude and longitude are measured in. To give a sense of size: a tenth of a degree (0.1°) of latitude is about 11 kilometres, anywhere on Earth. A tenth of a degree of longitude is also about 11 kilometres near the equator, but gets shorter towards the poles."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Area-peril grid (grid)","anchor":"term-area-peril-grid-grid","card":"Grid terms","text":"A pattern of small rectangles laid over a country, like a sheet of graph paper over a map. The model works out the shaking once for each rectangle rather than separately for every building, which makes the calculation fast enough to be practical. Every building inside a rectangle is given that rectangle's shaking. The name comes from the loss engine, which calls each rectangle an “area peril”."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Cell","anchor":"term-cell","card":"Grid terms","text":"One rectangle of the grid. Its edges are two lines of latitude (its south and north sides) and two lines of longitude (its west and east sides)."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"AreaPerilID","anchor":"term-areaperilid","card":"Grid terms","text":"The number that identifies a cell: 1, 2, 3 and so on. Other parts of CASS, such as the stored shaking, refer to cells by this number. Within one version of a grid, a number always means the same rectangle on the map."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Resolution","anchor":"term-resolution","card":"Grid terms","text":"How big the cells are, measured in degrees. A smaller number means smaller cells, which gives more detail but means many more cells to calculate. Halving the resolution (for example going from 0.1° to 0.05°) makes four times as many cells."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Base resolution","anchor":"term-base-resolution","card":"Grid terms","text":"The cell size used across the whole grid, except in areas where a refinement asks for smaller cells."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Tile","anchor":"term-tile","card":"Grid terms","text":"A rectangle you draw around part of a country to say “include this area in the model”, with a name and a reason, such as “Java and Madura”. You can think of a tile as a window: it decides which part of the graph paper is used. A tile does not have to follow the coast, because the grid can leave out the sea by itself (see Land clip)."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Domain","anchor":"term-domain","card":"Grid terms","text":"The whole area the grid covers: all of its tiles put together, less any cells left out as sea or as empty land. A property whose coordinates fall outside the domain cannot be modelled, and CASS lists it so you know."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Refinement","anchor":"term-refinement","card":"Grid terms","text":"An area, usually a city, where you want more detail, so the grid uses smaller cells there than elsewhere. For example, the Indonesian grid uses cells about 11 km across in most places but about 2.8 km across in Jakarta, where a lot of insured value is concentrated and the ground changes a lot over short distances. The small cells take the place of the larger ones in that area; they are not added on top."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Grid specification","anchor":"term-grid-specification","card":"Grid terms","text":"The written instructions for a grid: which country, which version, the base resolution, the tiles, the refinements, and a reason for each. You write the specification; CASS creates the cells from it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Mapping tolerance","anchor":"term-mapping-tolerance","card":"Grid terms","text":"A distance in kilometres saved with a grid. It records how close a property just outside the tiles would need to be before it would be acceptable to treat it as being inside the nearest cell. CASS does not currently move any property into a cell it is not in; it always reports such properties instead. So in practice this number is a note for the future, and 0 is the normal value."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Cell limit","anchor":"term-cell-limit","card":"Grid terms","text":"The largest number of cells this installation will build in one grid: 500,000, unless an administrator has changed it. Every cell has its shaking calculated and stored for every imagined year, so the limit keeps the hazard calculation and its storage practical. The grid itself is quick to build and use at this size."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Seed grid","anchor":"term-seed-grid","card":"Grid terms","text":"A ready-made grid specification CASS ships for a country: Bangladesh, Bhutan, Indonesia, Kuwait, the Maldives, Nepal, Oman, the Philippines, Qatar and Türkiye. You load it into the grid form, change what you want, and build it. Every seed follows the same rule for cell size and says what it leaves open."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Land clip","anchor":"term-land-clip","card":"Grid terms","text":"The option “Keep only cells that touch the country's land”. CASS compares each cell with the country's outline from Natural Earth, a free world map, and leaves out cells entirely over the sea or in another country. A cell that touches land at all is kept, so coasts are not lost."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Coast buffer","anchor":"term-coast-buffer","card":"Grid terms","text":"How far out to sea the land clip still keeps cells: 5 km unless you change it. The world map's coastlines are accurate to about 5 km, and an address can be placed just offshore."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Settlement buffer","anchor":"term-settlement-buffer","card":"Grid terms","text":"How far from any building or resident a cell can be and still be kept, when the grid skips land with no buildings or people nearby: 5 km unless you change it. At 5 km, none of KRE's geocoded locations were left out. Widen it if your book has isolated sites, such as mines or dams, far from towns."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"Hazard terms","anchor":"card-hazard-terms","card":"","text":"The earthquake side of the model: how often earthquakes happen, and how hard each one shakes a place."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Hazard model","anchor":"term-hazard-model","card":"Hazard terms","text":"An earthquake model for a country, published by scientists. It describes where earthquakes can happen, how large they can be, how often they happen, and how the shaking spreads out and weakens with distance. It arrives as a zip file in OpenQuake's format, which you upload to CASS."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"job.ini","anchor":"term-job-ini","card":"Hazard terms","text":"A settings file inside the hazard model's zip file. It tells OpenQuake how to run the model: for example, how many years to imagine. CASS reads it and shows you its settings on screen, so you do not need to open it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Logic tree","anchor":"term-logic-tree","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Earthquake scientists do not always agree. For example, they may disagree about how quickly shaking weakens with distance. Instead of choosing one answer, a hazard model lists the reasonable alternatives and gives each a weight showing how much the scientists trust it. This list of alternatives and weights is called a logic tree, because it branches like a tree at each question."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Path (realisation)","anchor":"term-path-realisation","card":"Hazard terms","text":"One route through the logic tree, choosing one alternative at every branch. Each path is one complete, reasonable view of the hazard. CASS picks 20 paths at random, in proportion to their weights, and combines their earthquakes. Using 20 rather than 1 gives an answer much closer to the scientists' overall view."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Classical and event-based","anchor":"term-classical-and-event-based","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Two different kinds of calculation OpenQuake can do. A classical calculation answers “what is the chance of each level of shaking at this place?”, but produces no individual earthquakes. An event-based calculation imagines individual earthquakes one by one. A loss model needs individual earthquakes, because one earthquake damages many buildings at once and those losses have to be added together. Published models are usually set up as classical, so CASS always switches them to event-based."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Investigation time","anchor":"term-investigation-time","card":"Hazard terms","text":"The number of years covered by one batch of imagined earthquakes. The default in CASS is 50 years."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Stochastic event set (SES)","anchor":"term-stochastic-event-set-ses","card":"Hazard terms","text":"One batch of imagined years of earthquakes (“stochastic” means generated at random). The total number of imagined years is: the investigation time × the number of batches per path × the number of paths. With the defaults that is 50 × 1 × 20 = 1,000 years."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Intensity measure (IMT)","anchor":"term-intensity-measure-imt","card":"Hazard terms","text":"A way of measuring how strong shaking is. There are several, because different buildings respond to different kinds of shaking. PGA (peak ground acceleration) measures how violently the ground itself moves. SA (spectral acceleration), written with a number such as SA(0.3), measures how hard a building that naturally sways back and forth once every 0.3 seconds would be pushed. Short, stiff buildings sway quickly and respond to short numbers; tall buildings sway slowly and respond to longer ones. Each vulnerability function says which intensity measure it needs."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"g","anchor":"term-g","card":"Hazard terms","text":"The unit shaking strength is measured in: the acceleration caused by Earth's gravity. Shaking of 0.2 g pushes the ground (or the building) with a force of one fifth of gravity. Strong earthquake shaking near a fault can exceed 1 g."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Intensity bin","anchor":"term-intensity-bin","card":"Hazard terms","text":"A band of shaking strength, for example from 0.20 g to 0.25 g. To keep the stored data manageable, the loss engine records which band the shaking falls in rather than its exact value."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Clipped hazard","anchor":"term-clipped-hazard","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Shaking stronger than the top band can hold. When that happens the extra strength is cut off, so the model would underestimate damage. Because these are the very strongest shakes, and so the ones causing the biggest losses, CASS will not publish a model version with clipped hazard as a finished model."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Footprint","anchor":"term-footprint","card":"Hazard terms","text":"The stored record of how hard each imagined earthquake shakes each cell. There is one footprint for each intensity measure. It is usually the largest part of a model, so CASS stores it compressed."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Occurrence table","anchor":"term-occurrence-table","card":"Hazard terms","text":"A list saying which imagined year each earthquake happens in. It lets CASS add up the losses from all the earthquakes in the same year."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Hazard set","anchor":"term-hazard-set","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Everything a finished hazard calculation produces, stored together: the footprints, the occurrence table, the intensity bands, and a record of the calculation that made them. It is made once for a grid and reused by every portfolio on that grid."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Datastore","anchor":"term-datastore","card":"Hazard terms","text":"The full results of an OpenQuake calculation: every imagined earthquake and the exact strength of its shaking in every cell, before it is sorted into intensity bands. CASS keeps it for 90 days after the calculation, and removes OpenQuake's own copy so it is only stored once."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Rebuild a footprint","anchor":"term-rebuild-a-footprint","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Sorting a hazard set's stored shaking into CASS's current intensity bands, when the bands have changed since the set was made. It reads the datastore, so the earthquakes do not have to be calculated again. The result is a new hazard set; the old one's footprint is deleted once no model version uses it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Region","anchor":"term-region","card":"Hazard terms","text":"The part of the grid a hazard calculation covers. Calculating the whole grid of a large country can take many hours. Choosing a smaller region, such as Jakarta and Bandung, finishes in minutes, but only properties inside that region will get a loss."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Site conditions and Vs30","anchor":"term-site-conditions-and-vs30","card":"Hazard terms","text":"Site conditions describe the ground beneath a place. Soft ground, such as the thick clay under many cities, makes shaking stronger than hard rock does. Vs30 is the usual measure: it is how fast a certain kind of vibration travels through the top 30 metres of ground. A low Vs30 means soft ground; a high one means rock. Where the hazard model has no Vs30 measurement within 15 km of a cell, CASS treats that cell as rock, which makes the shaking, and so the loss, lower than it may really be on soft ground."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"Vulnerability and model terms","anchor":"card-vulnerability-and-model-terms","card":"","text":"The building-damage side of the model, and putting a whole model together."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Vulnerability function","anchor":"term-vulnerability-function","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A curve for one type of building that gives the expected damage at each strength of shaking. The damage is given as a percentage of the cost to rebuild. For example, a function might say that at a certain level of shaking, a building of that type suffers on average 10% damage, and at twice that shaking, 35%."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Vulnerability set","anchor":"term-vulnerability-set","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"All the vulnerability functions for one country, together with the choices used to pick between them. It is built from GEM's published data plus the design-era information you provide. When you set up a run, the same thing is called the assumption set, because it contains the assumptions about buildings whose details are unknown."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"GEM release","anchor":"term-gem-release","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A downloaded copy of GEM's published data, kept on the computer CASS runs on. GEM publishes new releases from time to time. v2026.0.0 is the release CASS has been checked against."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Building class (taxonomy)","anchor":"term-building-class-taxonomy","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A type of building, described by three things: what it is used for (occupancy, such as a home, shop or factory), what it is built from (construction, such as reinforced concrete or brick), and how tall it is. A taxonomy is the naming system used to describe these types."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Building stock","anchor":"term-building-stock","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"All the buildings in a country. GEM estimates what share of a country's buildings are of each type. CASS uses that mix when a property's own details are missing."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Seismic design level","anchor":"term-seismic-design-level","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"How well a building was designed to withstand earthquakes. Countries introduce and strengthen earthquake building codes over time, so buildings of different ages can have very different levels of protection. GEM uses four levels: CDN (no seismic design), CDL (low), CDM (moderate) and CDH (high). GEM publishes a separate vulnerability function for each level."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Design era","anchor":"term-design-era","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A period of years together with the seismic design levels a building from that period might have, and the reason for thinking so. For example, you might say buildings up to 2002 have no or low design, because the country's modern earthquake code was introduced that year. A table of eras lets CASS use a property's year of construction to choose the right vulnerability functions."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Enrichment","anchor":"term-enrichment","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"The information you add to GEM's data when you build a vulnerability set: the design eras, the reason for each, the stock weighting, and any questions still unanswered. You give it a name and a version. The set is named by the version, with -gem added — an enrichment at 0.1.0-draft builds a set 0.1.0-draft-gem — so building again under the same version replaces that set rather than adding another. Raise the version whenever you change what the enrichment says."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Stock weighting","anchor":"term-stock-weighting","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"How CASS weights the mix of building types when it needs to blend them. By replacement cost, building types count in proportion to what they would cost to rebuild, so expensive buildings count for more. By building count, each building counts once, however valuable. Replacement cost is usually the better choice for insurance losses, because losses are about money."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Channel (sub-peril item)","anchor":"term-channel-sub-peril-item","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"Sometimes one building class includes buildings that respond to different intensity measures (for example, a mix of short and tall buildings when the height is unknown). CASS then splits the class into parts, one for each intensity measure, and gives each part its share of the class. Each part is called a channel. This happens automatically."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Model version","anchor":"term-model-version","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A grid, a vulnerability set and a hazard set for one country, joined together and given their own version number. It is the complete model a run uses. Giving it a version means a result can always be traced back to exactly which model produced it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Research prototype","anchor":"term-research-prototype","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A model version that has been published even though some work on it is unfinished. It can be run, and its results are useful for research, but its results cannot be approved. The unfinished items are listed on the model version."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Publication blocker","anchor":"term-publication-blocker","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"One unfinished item that stops a model version being published as a finished model. CASS lists every blocker on the model version, so you can see exactly what is missing rather than having to guess."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Oasis package","anchor":"term-oasis-package","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A model version converted into the files the Oasis loss engine reads. The engine can only use one package at a time, so building a package for one model version replaces the previous one."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Gate (approval)","anchor":"term-gate-approval","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A point where CASS requires a second person to check and agree before work can continue. One person asks for approval, and a reviewer who is not that person approves or rejects it. Nothing is lost while work waits at a gate."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Converter-candidate gate","anchor":"term-converter-candidate-gate","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"The gate that must be approved before an Oasis package can be built. It is the reviewer's agreement to the way the hazard and vulnerability are converted into Oasis's format."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Draft and published","anchor":"term-draft-and-published","card":"Vulnerability and model terms","text":"A draft can still be changed. Once published, an item is frozen and can never change, so anything calculated from it can be repeated exactly. If a published item needs correcting, CASS makes a new version instead."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"Portfolio and insurance terms","anchor":"card-portfolio-and-insurance-terms","card":"","text":"Your list of properties, and the insurance and reinsurance that sits on it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Project","anchor":"term-project","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A folder for one piece of work. It holds the portfolios, the imports, the runs and the results that belong together. Anyone can create a project, whatever their role, and the person who creates it owns it; everybody else sees it only once they are made a member. A project does three jobs, and each one matters at a different moment. It decides who can see the work. Membership of the project, not your role, is what grants access to a portfolio or a result. It names the files. The short reference you give it labels every file the project stores, which is why it cannot be changed afterwards and why it is worth keeping short. It is the unit of deletion. Delete a project and its portfolios, imports, runs and results go with it. What a project does not hold is the model. Grids, vulnerability sets, hazard sets and model versions belong to the whole installation, not to any project: your run borrows one, your colleague's project uses the same one, and deleting your project leaves it untouched."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Portfolio (exposure version)","anchor":"term-portfolio-exposure-version","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Your list of insured properties, as CASS stores it. Each time it is changed, CASS keeps the old version and makes a new numbered one (version 1, version 2 and so on). A run always uses one exact, published version, so its results can be traced to the data that produced them."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Schedule","anchor":"term-schedule","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The insurer's own list of insured properties, usually a spreadsheet. It is what you bring into CASS to make a portfolio."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"OED","anchor":"term-oed","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Open Exposure Data: a standard layout for insurance property data, used across the industry so different software reads the same columns in the same way. CASS stores every portfolio as OED files: one for locations, one for accounts and policies, and files for reinsurance."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Intake template","anchor":"term-intake-template","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A spreadsheet provided by CASS for bringing in a schedule that is not already in OED. You download it, copy your data into its columns, and upload it. Its columns are exactly the ones CASS reads."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Location","anchor":"term-location","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"One insured property: its address and coordinates, its building details, and its values."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Account and policy","anchor":"term-account-and-policy","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"An account is one insured customer, who may have several locations. A policy is the insurance contract that customer holds, setting out what is covered, the deductible and the limits. Each policy has a Policy ID, which CASS uses to link locations to their policy."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Deductible","anchor":"term-deductible","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The part of a loss the policyholder pays themselves before the insurance pays anything. For example, with a deductible of 20,000 and damage of 120,000, the insurance pays 100,000."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Limit","anchor":"term-limit","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The most the insurance will pay. For example, with a limit of 500,000, damage of 800,000 is paid only up to 500,000."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Layer and attachment","anchor":"term-layer-and-attachment","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Insurance is sometimes arranged in slices, one above another, called layers. A layer starts paying once the loss reaches its attachment point, and stops at its limit. For example, a layer attaching at 1,000,000 with a limit of 4,000,000 pays the part of a loss between 1,000,000 and 5,000,000."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Share","anchor":"term-share","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The proportion of a layer or contract that is taken, written as a decimal: 0.25 means 25%, and 1 means all of it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"TIV (total insured value)","anchor":"term-tiv-total-insured-value","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The total value insured, either at one location or across the whole portfolio."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Coverage","anchor":"term-coverage","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Which kind of value is insured. There are four: the building itself, other structures (such as a separate garage), the contents inside, and business interruption (income lost while the building cannot be used)."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Peril code","anchor":"term-peril-code","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A short code in OED saying which perils a policy covers. QEQ means earthquake shaking only, QQ1 means all earthquake perils (shaking, and also tsunami, fire following, liquefaction and landslide), and AA1 means every peril of every kind. Which one to write depends on the route. In the intake template write QEQ: promotion records QEQ whatever you type. In your own OED files write what is true of the policy, and CASS reads it as OED defines it. Either way CASS models shaking only."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Class of business","anchor":"term-class-of-business","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"What kind of insurance a row belongs to. CASS includes Fire (property) rows, and leaves out Liability, which insures no building at the coordinates, and Engineering, which needs different vulnerability treatment. The match is exact, so fire in lower case is left out too."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Geocode precision (the column)","anchor":"term-geocode-precision-the-column","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The intake template's column for how exact a geocode is. It takes one of six words: parcel, street or embedded for a location exact enough to trust its cell, and locality, postcode or admin for an approximate one. Anything else, blank included, makes the row unclassified."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Geocode","anchor":"term-geocode","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The latitude and longitude found for an address. How exact it is depends on how much of the address could be matched: a geocode to the exact building (rooftop) is precise, while one that could only be matched to a postcode or town is really just the middle of that area. A coarse geocode can put a property in the wrong cell."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Import","anchor":"term-import","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A spreadsheet that has been uploaded, read and checked, but not yet turned into a portfolio. It becomes a portfolio when you promote it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Cohort","anchor":"term-cohort","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The group CASS puts each imported location into, which says what has to happen before it can be used. There are four, and the screens name the first three rather than lettering them. A, shown as Automated test cohort: precise geocode, nothing to check, ready to use. B, shown as Geocoding-sensitivity cohort: nothing to check, but the geocode is approximate, so the cell it lands in may be wrong. C, shown as Analyst-review backlog: a person needs to look at it. Unclassified is the fourth, and the one that catches people out. A row lands there when it has no coordinates, when they are 0 and 0, when they fall outside the country the row names, when CASS has no check for that country (only Indonesia and Nepal today), or when its Geocode precision is blank or a word CASS does not recognise. Unclassified rows are not offered when promoting and can never be included, so a template filled in without Geocode precision produces an import that cannot become a portfolio."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Promote","anchor":"term-promote","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Turn a checked import into a portfolio. When you promote, you choose which cohort to include and what CASS should assume where information is missing. A customer is taken whole or not at all: if one of an account's properties does not qualify, none of that account is included, because splitting a schedule would leave its policy value with nowhere honest to go."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Allocation","anchor":"term-allocation","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"How a policy's total value is shared out between its locations, when the schedule gives only one total for several locations. The simplest choice, and the starting point, is to share it equally."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Coverage split","anchor":"term-coverage-split","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"How a single stated value is divided between building, contents and business interruption, when the schedule does not say. It is only used when the schedule is silent."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Finding","anchor":"term-finding","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A problem CASS has found in the data, such as a missing value or a value that does not make sense. An error must be fixed before the portfolio can be published. A warning does not stop publication, but it is kept with the portfolio and shown with its results."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Geocoding sensitivity","anchor":"term-geocoding-sensitivity","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A check on the coarse geocodes. CASS moves each such property around the area its geocode could really mean (for example, anywhere in the postcode) and sees whether it would still fall in the same cell. It reports how much insured value sits in a cell that cannot be relied on."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Financial structure","anchor":"term-financial-structure","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"All the insurance and reinsurance terms attached to a portfolio: the policies, and the reinsurance contracts."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Reinsurance, treaty and ceded","anchor":"term-reinsurance-treaty-and-ceded","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"Reinsurance is insurance for insurers: an insurer pays a reinsurer to take on part of its losses. A treaty is a reinsurance contract. The part of a loss passed to the reinsurer is said to be ceded."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Quota share (QS)","anchor":"term-quota-share-qs","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A treaty where the reinsurer pays a fixed percentage of every loss it covers. With a 30% quota share, a loss of 100,000 costs the reinsurer 30,000 and the insurer 70,000."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Surplus share (SS)","anchor":"term-surplus-share-ss","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A treaty that works one property (risk) at a time. The insurer keeps up to a set amount on each risk, and the reinsurer takes a share of the larger risks, set separately for each risk named in the treaty."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Catastrophe excess of loss (CXL)","anchor":"term-catastrophe-excess-of-loss-cxl","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"A treaty that protects against one very large event. It adds up everything one earthquake costs across the portfolio and pays the part above a set amount (the attachment), up to a maximum (the limit). For example, a treaty of 2,000,000 over 100,000 pays the part of each event's loss between 100,000 and 2,100,000."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Inuring priority","anchor":"term-inuring-priority","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The order in which treaties are applied when there is more than one. The treaty with priority 1 is applied first, and the next treaty only sees the loss that is left after it. The order can change the result considerably."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Placed share","anchor":"term-placed-share","card":"Portfolio and insurance terms","text":"The part of a treaty actually sold to reinsurers. If only 80% of a treaty was placed, only 80% of what it would pay is recovered."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"card","label":"Run and result terms","anchor":"card-run-and-result-terms","card":"","text":"Calculating the losses, and reading the numbers that come out."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Run (analysis run)","anchor":"term-run-analysis-run","card":"Run and result terms","text":"One calculation of one portfolio version through one model version. Each run is kept, with a record of exactly what it used."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Run mode","anchor":"term-run-mode","card":"Run and result terms","text":"What a run is for, which decides how far it goes and what its results may be used for. Geometry only just checks which cell each property falls in, and calculates no loss. KRE-share technical loss and Portfolio-loss research calculate losses for research. Decision use produces a result that a reviewer may approve."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Stage","anchor":"term-stage","card":"Run and result terms","text":"One step of a run, such as reading the portfolio or calculating the losses. The Run monitor shows each stage as it happens."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Keys","anchor":"term-keys","card":"Run and result terms","text":"The stage that matches each property to its cell and to the vulnerability function for its type of building. The name comes from the loss engine: each match is a “key” that unlocks the right hazard and damage data for that property."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Keys reconciliation","anchor":"term-keys-reconciliation","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A check that every part of the portfolio's value has been accounted for. Each part must be either matched, deliberately not modelled, or reported as not matched. If some value could not be matched, the run pauses until a person agrees it should continue, so value never silently goes missing from the total."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"fail_ap, fail_v and notatrisk","anchor":"term-fail-ap-fail-v-and-notatrisk","card":"Run and result terms","text":"Labels the keys stage gives properties it could not simply match. fail_ap: the property is outside the grid (“ap” is short for area peril). fail_v: no vulnerability function fits the building (“v” for vulnerability). notatrisk: deliberately left out, for example a coverage with no value."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Exception","anchor":"term-exception","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A request, with a written reason, for a paused run to continue. A reviewer who did not make the request decides whether to allow it."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Smoke check","anchor":"term-smoke-check","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A quick trial run using only a few of the largest earthquakes, done before the full calculation. It catches a broken setup in minutes rather than after a long run. (The name comes from electronics: switch it on and see if smoke comes out.)"},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Resource profile","anchor":"term-resource-profile","card":"Run and result terms","text":"How much computing power a run is given: the number of processors, the memory and the time limit."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Artifact","anchor":"term-artifact","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A file a run produced, such as its input files or raw outputs. Artifacts are kept with the run so they can be downloaded and checked later."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Result","anchor":"term-result","card":"Run and result terms","text":"The losses from one run for one perspective, stored together with everything they depend on: the model version, the portfolio version and the choices made."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Average annual loss (AAL)","anchor":"term-average-annual-loss-aal","card":"Run and result terms","text":"The loss to expect in an average year. It is the total loss across all the imagined years divided by the number of years. For example, if 1,000 imagined years produce 5,000,000 of loss in total, the AAL is 5,000. Most years will have far less than this, and a few will have much more. The AAL is the most reliable figure a run produces."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Standard deviation","anchor":"term-standard-deviation","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A measure of how much the yearly loss varies from one year to the next. A large standard deviation compared with the AAL means losses come mainly in occasional very bad years."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Return period","anchor":"term-return-period","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A way of describing how rare a loss is. The 100-year loss is the loss that is exceeded, on average, once every 100 years, which is a 1% chance in any one year. It does not mean the loss happens exactly once a century: it could happen twice in ten years, or not at all for three hundred."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Exceedance probability (EP) curve","anchor":"term-exceedance-probability-ep-curve","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A chart showing, for each size of loss, the chance of a loss at least that large in any one year. Rare, large losses are at one end and common, small ones at the other. The loss at each return period is read from this curve."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Event loss table","anchor":"term-event-loss-table","card":"Run and result terms","text":"A list of each imagined earthquake and the loss it caused. It shows which earthquakes drive the result."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Scenario range","anchor":"term-scenario-range","card":"Run and result terms","text":"The same calculation repeated under different assumption sets, to show how much the answer depends on those assumptions."},
  {"section":"glossary","sectionLabel":"Glossary","kind":"term","label":"Comparison","anchor":"term-comparison","card":"Run and result terms","text":"Two results side by side, showing how the numbers differ and what changed between the two runs to explain it."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"Why a model needs a grid","anchor":"card-why-a-model-needs-a-grid","card":"","text":"To calculate a loss, CASS needs to know how hard the ground shakes under every property, for every one of thousands of imagined earthquakes. Calculating that separately for each address would take far too long, and earthquake science is not precise enough for the extra detail to help. So instead, CASS covers the country with a pattern of small rectangles, like a sheet of graph paper laid over a map. Each rectangle is called a cell. The shaking is calculated once for each cell, for each earthquake. Every property inside a cell is then given that cell's shaking. The whole pattern of cells is the grid . A grid is built once for a country, before any portfolio is brought in, and then kept fixed. That matters: every portfolio and every earthquake calculation for that country uses exactly the same cells, so the results of two runs can be fairly compared. You do not draw the cells one by one. You write a short set of instructions, called a specification, saying which areas to cover and how big the cells should be, and CASS creates the cells from it. You do not have to write a specification from nothing either. CASS ships a ready-made one, called a seed, for ten countries: Bangladesh, Bhutan, Indonesia, Kuwait, the Maldives, Nepal, Oman, the Philippines, Qatar and Türkiye. You load a seed into the form, change anything you want, and build it. The seeds are described further down this page."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"What a cell is","anchor":"card-what-a-cell-is","card":"","text":"A rectangle drawn with lines of latitude and longitude. Its south and north edges are lines of latitude (the minimum and maximum latitude), and its west and east edges are lines of longitude (the minimum and maximum longitude). Those four numbers are all it takes to describe a cell. Measured in degrees, not kilometres. A cell 0.1° tall is always about 11.1 km tall. A cell 0.1° wide is about 11.1 km wide at the equator, but narrower further north or south, because lines of longitude get closer together towards the poles. In Jakarta, close to the equator, it is about 11.0 km wide, so the cells are nearly square. Far from the equator they are taller than they are wide. Numbered. Each cell has a number, its AreaPerilID. Other parts of CASS, such as the stored shaking, refer to a cell by its number. Never overlapping. Every point on the map is in exactly one cell. A point that lies exactly on the line between two cells is counted in the cell to its north or east, so there is never any doubt. Only a shape, to begin with. A newly built cell contains no shaking and no information about the ground. Shaking is added later, when a hazard calculation is run on the grid."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"The four parts of a specification","anchor":"card-the-four-parts-of-a-specification","card":"","text":"A specification says four things: which areas to include (the tiles), how big the cells should be (the base resolution), where to use smaller cells (the refinements), and which cells to leave out because nothing insured can be in them (what the grid keeps). The picture shows the first three."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"term","label":"1. Tiles: which areas to include","anchor":"term-1-tiles-which-areas-to-include","card":"The four parts of a specification","text":"A tile is a rectangle you draw around part of the country to say “include this area”. You describe it with four numbers (its minimum and maximum latitude and longitude), a name, and a short reason. All the tiles together make up the area the grid covers, called the domain. Why tiles exist. Tiles let you name the parts of a country you are modelling, and leave out parts you are not. The Indonesian seed, for example, has ten tiles, such as “Sumatra”, “Java and Madura” and “Sangihe, Talaud and Miangas”. Each has a reason a reviewer can read. Tiles do not have to follow the coast. A rectangle drawn around islands always holds a lot of sea: the eight rectangles of the first Indonesian grid held 52,831 cells, and only about 18,000 of them touched land. You could try to follow the coast with many small rectangles, but doing that for Indonesia takes about 2,000 of them, which nobody could check. Instead, draw a few simple rectangles and let the grid leave out the sea for you (part 4 below). A helpful way to picture it. Imagine one sheet of graph paper covering the whole world. Laying a tile is like cutting a window in a piece of card and placing it on top: the window decides which squares are used, but it never moves the squares themselves. What happens outside the tiles. An area outside every tile simply has no cells. A property there cannot be given any shaking, so CASS reports it as outside the domain. It never quietly moves the property into the nearest cell, because that would give it the wrong shaking without anyone knowing. Tiles are allowed to touch or overlap. Where two tiles overlap, each cell is still only created once."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"term","label":"2. Base resolution: how big the cells are","anchor":"term-2-base-resolution-how-big-the-cells-are","card":"The four parts of a specification","text":"The size of the cells, in degrees, everywhere except where a refinement asks for smaller ones. A base resolution of 0.1° makes cells about 11 km across. This one number has the biggest effect on how many cells the grid has."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"term","label":"3. Refinements: where to use smaller cells","anchor":"term-3-refinements-where-to-use-smaller-cells","card":"The four parts of a specification","text":"A refinement is a rectangle where you want more detail, so the cells there are smaller than the base resolution. Like a tile, it has four numbers, a name and a reason, plus its own, smaller cell size. When to use one. Where larger cells would hide differences that matter: for example in a city with a lot of insured value, or where the ground changes quickly from hard rock to soft soil, so shaking can be very different a few kilometres apart. The Indonesian grid refines Jakarta, Bandung, Surabaya, Denpasar and Medan to 0.025°, about 2.8 km. How it fits in. The small cells replace the large cells in that area; they are not laid on top of them, so no place is ever in two cells. For that to work, a refinement has to fit exactly over whole large cells, and CASS checks three things: Its cells must be smaller than the base resolution, or it adds no detail. Its cell size must divide the base cell size a whole number of times. With a base of 0.1°, a refinement of 0.05° (2 across) or 0.025° (4 across) fits; 0.03° does not, because three and a bit small cells would not fill a large one. Its four edges must be whole multiples of the base cell size. With a base of 0.1°, edges at 106.5 and 107.1 are fine; an edge at 106.53 is not. What goes wrong otherwise. Before CASS checked this, a refinement drawn with edges at 0.06 and 0.14 over a 0.1° base put a quarter of the places inside it into two cells at once, and one drawn at 0.05 to 0.17 left three fifths of its area in no cell at all. Now CASS refuses such a refinement and tells you the nearest box that would fit, for example “latitude 0 to 0.2, longitude 0 to 0.2”."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"term","label":"4. What the grid keeps: land, and places near people or buildings","anchor":"term-4-what-the-grid-keeps-land-and-places-near-people-or-buildings","card":"The four parts of a specification","text":"Two tick boxes decide which of the tiles' cells are kept. Both are ticked when you start a new specification. Keep only cells that touch the country's land. CASS compares every cell with the country's outline and leaves out cells that are entirely over the sea, or entirely in another country. The outline comes from Natural Earth, a free world map that ships with CASS. A cell is kept if any part of it touches land, not just its middle: checking only the middle would have dropped 9% of Indonesia's cells, all of them on coasts, where many insured buildings are. The coast buffer keeps cells a little way out to sea as well, 5 km unless you change it. The map's coastlines are only accurate to about 5 km, and an address can be placed just offshore of a coastline drawn that way. Skip land with no buildings or people nearby. Land is not the same as places where insured property can be: the middle of a desert, a mountain range or a rainforest has no buildings. CASS reads a world map of buildings and residents, made from satellite images by the European Commission (the Global Human Settlement Layer), and leaves out cells that are further than the settlement buffer from any building or resident. Using both buildings and residents matters: a factory or a port can have buildings and no residents. The settlement buffer is 5 km unless you change it. That number was tested against the real locations in KRE's geocoded portfolio: at 2 km, two of 224 locations would have been left out; at 5 km, none were. What these cannot see. The outline leaves out many very small islands (it draws only 176 of the Maldives' roughly 1,190), and the building map can miss an isolated site such as a mine, a dam or a pipeline station far from any town. A property in a cell that was left out is reported as outside the grid, never moved or silently dropped. If your book has remote sites, check they are inside before relying on the grid."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","anchor":"card-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells","card":"","text":"You do not need to know this to build a grid, but it explains some things you may notice, such as a tile covering slightly more area than you typed. When you press Build the grid, CASS does the following."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Fill each refinement with small cells","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--fill-each-refinement-with-small-cells","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":1,"text":"For every refinement, CASS covers its rectangle with cells of the refinement's size."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Fill each tile with base cells, skipping refined areas","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--fill-each-tile-with-base-cells-skipping-refined-areas","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":2,"text":"For every tile, CASS covers its rectangle with cells of the base size. If a base cell's centre falls inside a refinement, that base cell is left out, because the refinement has already covered that ground with smaller cells."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Line every cell up on one worldwide pattern","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--line-every-cell-up-on-one-worldwide-pattern","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":3,"text":"Cell edges are always placed at whole multiples of the cell size, counted from latitude 0 and longitude 0, rather than starting from the corner of your tile. This is the graph paper from the picture above: the squares are always in the same places, whatever window you cut. For example, with 0.1° cells, cell edges fall at 106.0, 106.1, 106.2 and so on. If you type a tile edge of 106.03, the first cell still starts at 106.0, which is why a tile can cover slightly more ground than the numbers you typed. The benefit is that neighbouring tiles line up exactly instead of leaving thin gaps or slivers between them, and a refinement's small cells fit neatly inside the large ones."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Remove duplicates","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--remove-duplicates","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":4,"text":"Any cell covered by two overlapping tiles is kept only once."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Leave out the sea and empty land","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--leave-out-the-sea-and-empty-land","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":5,"text":"If the specification asks for it, CASS leaves out every cell that does not touch the country's land (allowing for the coast buffer), and every cell further than the settlement buffer from any building or resident. It counts how many cells each rule left out, and the build message tells you."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Put the cells in order and number them","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--put-the-cells-in-order-and-number-them","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":6,"text":"The cells are sorted from south to north, and from west to east within each row, then numbered 1, 2, 3 and so on, starting from the south-west corner. Because the order is always the same, the same specification always produces the same numbers."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Save and register the grid","anchor":"step-how-cass-turns-a-specification-into-cells--save-and-register-the-grid","card":"How CASS turns a specification into cells","step":7,"text":"The cells are saved as a table, with one row per cell giving its number, its four edges and its country. The grid is added to the list of grids as a draft. It is given a reference made from the country code, the word “grid” and the version, for example id-grid-1.0.0."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"How many cells, and why it matters","anchor":"card-how-many-cells-and-why-it-matters","card":"","text":"Every cell has to have its shaking calculated for every imagined earthquake, and that shaking has to be stored. So more cells means a longer hazard calculation, more storage, and a slower loss calculation. Choosing the cell size is a balance between detail and time. The number of cells grows faster than you might expect. Halving the cell size makes four times as many cells, because each old cell is split in half both across and down, into four. On the grid screen this is described as “cost is quadratic”. Cell counts at different resolutions Area covered Cell size Number of cells One tile, 2° by 2° (about 220 km square) 0.1° (about 11 km) 400 The same tile 0.05° (about 5.5 km) 1,600 The same tile 0.025° (about 2.8 km) 6,400 All of Indonesia 0.1° 52,831 All of Indonesia 0.01° (about 1 km) about 5,000,000 All of Indonesia, keeping only land near people or buildings (the seed) 0.025°, with ten cities at 0.0125° (about 1.4 km) 270,769 To help you judge this, the grid screen counts the cells while you type, so you can see the effect of a change before building anything. Once the specification is complete, the count is exact: it is the number the build will produce, after refinements have replaced large cells and the sea and empty land have been left out. This installation will not build a grid with more than 500,000 cells (unless an administrator has changed that limit), and the screen warns you before you reach it. Beside the count, the screen shows the most the grid's hazard could take up on disk for every 1,000 imagined years. For example, a grid of 100,000 cells shows “at most 2.8 GB for every thousand simulated years”, so a hazard run of the usual 10,000 years could store up to about 28 GB. The seed grid for all of Indonesia, 270,769 cells, could store up to about 77 GB. These are ceilings, measured on the Jakarta and Bandung area, which has the strongest shaking CASS has calculated; quieter areas store less. CASS only stores shaking of 0.05 g or more, because no building in the world earthquake model is damaged by less. The count is not a time estimate. On this installation, a hazard run of 10,000 imagined years for the 962 cells of Jakarta and Bandung took about 9 minutes from start to finish, but time does not grow simply with the number of cells, so CASS does not predict it for a whole country."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"Worked example: counting a tile, a refinement and the sea","anchor":"card-worked-example-counting-a-tile-a-refinement-and-the-sea","card":"","text":"One tile, one refinement, and a coast The tile runs from longitude 106.0 to 108.0 (2 degrees) and from latitude −7.5 to −5.5 (also 2 degrees), with cells of 0.1°. Two degrees divided by 0.1 is 20, so the tile has 20 columns and 20 rows: 400 cells. The refinement runs from longitude 106.6 to 107.0 and latitude −6.4 to −6.0 (0.4 degrees each way), with cells of 0.025°. 0.4 divided by 0.025 is 16, so it has 16 columns and 16 rows: 256 small cells. That same area held 4 columns and 4 rows of the large cells, which is 16 large cells, and those 16 are removed. Its edges (106.6, 107.0, −6.4 and −6.0) are all multiples of 0.1, and 0.025 goes into 0.1 exactly four times, so it fits. So far the grid has 400 − 16 + 256 = 640 cells. Now suppose the northern part of the tile is sea. With “Keep only cells that touch the country's land” ticked, CASS leaves out every cell that is entirely over the sea, including any more than 5 km from the coast. If that is 150 large cells, the counter shows 490 cells and says that 150 over the sea are left out. When the counter says “at most”. While the specification is still unfinished, or has a problem the build would refuse, CASS cannot count exactly. It then shows a number that can only be too high, never too low: it adds the 400 and the 256 without subtracting anything. That way, if it says you are under the limit, you are."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"Build a grid, step by step","anchor":"card-build-a-grid-step-by-step","card":"","text":"Who can do this, and where Catastrophe modellers and administrators can build grids. Go to Models → Build and find the card called Build a grid."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Start from a seed, if there is one","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--start-from-a-seed-if-there-is-one","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":1,"where":"Start from a country → Seed → Load into the form","text":"Start from a country → Seed → Load into the form If your country is one of the ten CASS ships a seed for, choose it under Seed and press Load into the form. Every field below is filled in, including the reasons and open questions. It replaces anything already in the form. Nothing is built until you press Build the grid, so you can change whatever you like first. If you use a seed, check each step below rather than skipping them."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Choose the country","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--choose-the-country","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":2,"where":"Country","text":"Country Start typing the country's name or its two-letter code, for example “Nepal” or “NP”, and pick it from the list. The list holds every country CASS has an outline for, which is what lets it leave out the sea. Countries with a seed say so."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Give it a version","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--give-it-a-version","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":3,"where":"Version","text":"Version Type a version, such as 1.0.0. You can use any text, but numbers that go up with each change are easiest to follow. Why the version matters. The stored shaking, model versions and past results all refer to cells by their numbers. If the shape of a grid changed but kept the same version, cell 1,204 might suddenly mean a different place, and everything built on the old grid would quietly point at the wrong ground. So whenever you change the tiles, the resolution or the refinements, use a new version. Building again with a version that already exists replaces that version's cells, so only do that for a grid nothing has been built on yet."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Give it a label","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--give-it-a-label","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":4,"where":"Label","text":"Label A name people will recognise in lists, such as “Indonesia national grid”."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Set the base resolution","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--set-the-base-resolution","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":5,"where":"Base resolution (degrees)","text":"Base resolution (degrees) Type the cell size in degrees as a decimal number, for example 0.1 for cells about 11 km across. It is saved exactly as you type it. If you are unsure, 0.1 is a reasonable size for a whole country; the table above shows how other sizes change the number of cells."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Leave the mapping tolerance at 0","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--leave-the-mapping-tolerance-at-0","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":6,"where":"Mapping tolerance (km)","text":"Mapping tolerance (km) This number is saved with the grid as a note, but it does not change anything today: CASS always reports properties outside the tiles rather than moving them into a nearby cell. Leave it at 0 unless you have been asked to record a particular value."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Choose what the grid keeps","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--choose-what-the-grid-keeps","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":7,"where":"What the grid keeps","text":"What the grid keeps Leave both boxes ticked unless you have a reason not to: Keep only cells that touch the country's land and Skip land with no buildings or people nearby. Leave both buffers at 5 km unless you have been asked to use something else. When to untick the land box. When the country's outline is known to be incomplete. The Maldives seed does this: the outline is missing most of its islands, so the seed relies on the buildings map instead. When to widen the settlement buffer. When your book has isolated sites far from towns, such as mines, dams, hydropower plants or oil and gas facilities. A wider buffer keeps more cells, and the counter shows how many."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Add the tiles","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--add-the-tiles","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":8,"where":"Tiles","text":"Tiles The form starts with one empty tile. For each tile, fill in: Name: where it is, such as “Java and Madura”. Reason: why this area is included, such as “Largest concentration of insured value”. A reviewer reads this later. Minimum latitude and maximum latitude: the southern and northern edges. Remember that south of the equator is negative. For a tile running from 9° south to 5.7° south, the minimum is -9.0 and the maximum is -5.7 (−9 is smaller than −5.7). Minimum longitude and maximum longitude: the western and eastern edges. East of Greenwich is positive, so for Indonesia both are positive numbers, with the minimum being the western edge. The minimum must always be smaller than the maximum. To add another tile, press Add a tile. The cell counter starts working as soon as one tile has all four numbers filled in. With the land box ticked, a tile can safely include sea and neighbouring countries, so draw it generously around the land you mean. Drawing it too tightly is the mistake to avoid: land left outside every tile has no cells. Tip: to find the latitude and longitude of a place, use any online map. Many let you right-click a point to copy its coordinates, given as latitude first, then longitude."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Add refinements, if you want any","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--add-refinements-if-you-want-any","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":9,"where":"Refinements","text":"Refinements Refinements are optional. To add one, press Add a refinement and fill in the same fields as a tile, plus its resolution: the smaller cell size. It must divide the base resolution exactly (for example 0.05 or 0.025 with a base of 0.1), and the four edges must be multiples of the base resolution (for example 106.5 and 107.1 with a base of 0.1). If they are not, the counter says so and suggests a box that would fit. Place refinements inside a tile. A refinement outside every tile still creates cells, which is rarely what you want."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Write down anything still undecided","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--write-down-anything-still-undecided","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":10,"where":"Open questions and Notes","text":"Open questions and Notes Under Open questions, write one question per line about anything the grid does not settle, such as “How should coastlines be handled?”. These are saved with the grid, so a later reader can tell the difference between something that was decided and something that was never considered. Use Notes for anything else worth recording."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Check the cell counter","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--check-the-cell-counter","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":11,"text":"Below the tick boxes, the screen shows how many cells your specification will make, split into base cells and the cells in each refinement, how many cells over the sea and on empty land were left out, and the most the grid's hazard could store. Look at it before building. A yellow warning called Some of the country's land is in no tile lists the approximate latitude and longitude of land your tiles missed, largest first. For example, “near 5.55, 126.6” is Miangas, a small Indonesian island north of Sulawesi. Widen a tile or add one to include it. A yellow warning called The build would refuse this or More cells than this installation builds means CASS would not build it, and says why. To reduce the number of cells, make the base resolution larger (for example 0.05 instead of 0.025), make the tiles smaller, or make the refinements cover less area."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"step","label":"Build it","anchor":"step-build-a-grid-step-by-step--build-it","card":"Build a grid, step by step","step":12,"where":"Build the grid","text":"Build the grid Press Build the grid. The button is only available once the country, version, label and base resolution are filled in and the specification is under the cell limit. A green message confirms the grid was built, with its reference, its number of cells, and how many cells over the sea and on empty land were left out. The grid then appears in the table Grids in the registry further down, marked as a draft. Building a grid does not approve it; it is simply ready to be used in a model version."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"The seed grids CASS ships","anchor":"card-the-seed-grids-cass-ships","card":"","text":"A seed is a complete specification for one country, written and checked when CASS was built. Every seed follows the same rule, so the choices behind them are the same everywhere: Cells of 0.0125° (about 1.4 km) wherever the country allows. That is about as fine as the world's soil data, so finer cells would add calculation without adding information. Cells of 0.025° (about 2.8 km) where 0.0125° would be too many. The test is whether the country's inhabited land fits in 300,000 cells, which leaves room under the 500,000 limit. Where it does not, the largest cities keep 1.4 km cells as refinements. Tiles that name regions, with the sea and empty land left out by the two tick boxes, each with a 5 km buffer. Each seed was checked to leave none of its country's land outside every tile. The seed grids Country Cell size Refined cities Cells Indonesia 0.025° Jakarta, Surabaya, Bandung, Medan, Semarang, Makassar, Palembang, Denpasar, Yogyakarta, Padang 270,769 Philippines 0.0125° None needed 220,318 Türkiye 0.025° Istanbul, Kocaeli, Izmir, Ankara, Bursa, Antalya, Adana, Gaziantep, Kahramanmaras, Antakya 144,678 Oman 0.0125° None needed 130,825 Bangladesh 0.0125° None needed 94,166 Nepal 0.0125° None needed 92,132 Bhutan 0.0125° None needed 24,583 Maldives 0.0125° None needed 16,185 Kuwait 0.0125° None needed 13,840 Qatar 0.0125° None needed 8,752 The Maldives is different. The world outline CASS uses draws only 176 of the Maldives' roughly 1,190 islands, so leaving out cells “not touching land” would drop real islands. Its seed leaves the land box unticked and keeps cells within 5 km of any building or resident instead. Its open questions also note that ground shaking there is low, and that the earthquake-related losses it has suffered came from tsunami, which CASS does not model. Every seed carries open questions like these, written for that country. Read them before using the grid."},
  {"section":"grids","sectionLabel":"Grids, cells and tiles","kind":"card","label":"Choosing tiles and a cell size","anchor":"card-choosing-tiles-and-a-cell-size","card":"","text":"There is no single correct grid, which is why CASS asks for a reason beside every tile and refinement: so others can understand and question your choices. These questions help. Is every insured property inside the grid? A property outside the grid cannot be modelled. The first Indonesian grid gained its Bangka Belitung tile only after a real property turned out to be in the gap between two tiles; now the counter warns about land outside every tile before you build. A property can also fall in a cell left out as empty land. A Geometry only run (see Run and read results) shows which properties fall outside, without the time and cost of a full loss calculation. Are the cells smaller than your addresses are accurate? If many properties are only located to a postcode or town, making the cells smaller than that area does not make their losses more accurate; it only adds cells. The geocoding sensitivity report in the import review shows how much value this affects. Where does the shaking change quickly? In places where soft ground sits next to hard rock, such as the basins under Jakarta and Bandung, shaking can be very different only a few kilometres apart. Those are good places for a refinement. Is the refinement based on the ground, or on today's portfolio? Refining around where your current properties happen to be is a reasonable start. But the grid is meant to stay fixed while portfolios change, so refinements based on where the ground or the shaking changes quickly will stay useful for longer. How much can the hazard take up? Every cell's shaking is stored for every imagined year, so a finer grid costs disk space for as long as its hazard is kept. The counter's storage figure is the most it could be. Once a hazard calculation has been run on a grid, changing the grid means running the hazard again (see Build a model), so settle the grid first. How much detail can the data support? The world data about soil and ground conditions is about 1 km across at its finest, and a hazard model's own site data is often coarser, 10 km for the Indonesian model. Cells much smaller than about 1 km add calculation without adding real information."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Before you start","anchor":"card-before-you-start","card":"","text":"A model version is the complete earthquake model for one country. It is made of three parts, and this section builds them in order: a grid, which divides the country into cells; a vulnerability set, which says how much damage shaking does to each type of building; a hazard set, which holds the imagined earthquakes and how hard each one shakes every cell. The grid and the vulnerability set each take a few minutes. The hazard set comes from a calculation that can take from a few minutes to several hours, depending on how much of the country it covers. Who can do this Only catastrophe modellers and administrators can see the Hazard and Build tabs. Step 8 also needs a reviewer. Everyone can see the Catalogue tab, which lists the model versions available to run. The order to work in, with the reason for it: Choose the GEM release, because the vulnerability set is built from it. Build the grid, because the hazard is calculated on its cells. Build the vulnerability set. Assemble the model version from the grid and the vulnerability set. Upload and run the hazard model on the same grid. Attach the hazard set the calculation produced to the model version. Publish the model version, so it can be chosen for runs. Get approval and build the Oasis package, which is the form the loss engine can read."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 1: Choose the GEM release","anchor":"card-part-1-choose-the-gem-release","card":"","text":"CASS takes its information about buildings and their vulnerability from GEM's published data, which is kept as a folder on the computer CASS runs on. This part tells CASS which copy to use. It usually only needs doing once. If no release is chosen CASS cannot build a vulnerability set, and the vulnerability card will say that no GEM release is configured."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Find the GEM release card","anchor":"step-part-1-choose-the-gem-release--find-the-gem-release-card","card":"Part 1: Choose the GEM release","step":1,"where":"Models → Build → GEM release","text":"Models → Build → GEM release The card lists every GEM release CASS has found. For each one it shows how many countries it contains and whether it is v2026.0.0, the release CASS has been checked against. A release marked as unusable cannot be chosen, and the card says why."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the release","anchor":"step-part-1-choose-the-gem-release--choose-the-release","card":"Part 1: Choose the GEM release","step":2,"where":"Use this release","text":"Use this release Press Use this release beside the one you want. The button changes to In use. The change takes effect straight away; nothing needs restarting. If the release you want is not listed, type its folder location in Or name the folder and press Use this folder. Ask your administrator for the location if you do not know it."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 2: Build the grid","anchor":"card-part-2-build-the-grid","card":"","text":"Use the Build a grid card on the Build tab. Grids have their own section in this guide, which explains what tiles and cells are and walks through every field: Grids, cells and tiles."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","anchor":"card-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set","card":"","text":"GEM publishes, for each country, the types of buildings found there, how common each type is, and vulnerability functions for each type. What GEM cannot know is how well buildings of different ages were designed for earthquakes, because that depends on when that country introduced and enforced its building codes. This part combines GEM's data with that local knowledge, which you provide."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the country","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--choose-the-country","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":1,"where":"Models → Build → Build a vulnerability set → Country in the GEM release","text":"Models → Build → Build a vulnerability set → Country in the GEM release Click the box and start typing the country's name or its ISO code, then choose it from the list. The list comes from the GEM release itself. The country codes are then filled in automatically from GEM's data, so the set cannot be saved under the wrong country by mistake. 208 of the 215 countries in GEM v2026.0.0 can be built. Seven cannot: the United States, Canada, Puerto Rico, the US Virgin Islands, Guam, American Samoa and the Northern Mariana Islands. They appear greyed out with the reason. GEM describes the buildings in those places using a different classification system (called HAZUS), and CASS does not yet have a way to translate it."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Name the enrichment and give it a version","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--name-the-enrichment-and-give-it-a-version","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":2,"where":"Enrichment name and Enrichment version","text":"Enrichment name and Enrichment version The enrichment is everything you state on this form that GEM does not supply: the design eras and the reason for each, or your stated reason for having none, the stock weighting, and the open questions. These two boxes label that set of assumptions so that a loss can later be traced back to it. They are the only two fields here you have to fill in yourself. Enrichment name is a label for the assumptions, and any text will do. Something short that says what they rest on reads best: is_design_eras for a set built on Iceland's code history. The two countries CASS was piloted on use id_gem_stock and np_gem_stock. The name is recorded with the build and in the audit trail; it is the version, below, that names the set afterwards. Enrichment version becomes the version of the vulnerability set, with -gem added to show the functions came from GEM. Type 0.1.0-draft and the set is listed as 0.1.0-draft-gem — which is how you will recognise it in Assemble a model version and in the catalogue. Keep it shorter than 28 characters, and prefer numbers you can raise, such as 0.1.0 then 0.2.0. Building again under the same version replaces the set A set is identified by its country and version together, so building Iceland again at 0.1.0-draft overwrites the Iceland set of that version rather than adding a second one. The functions change underneath anything already built on it, and there is no copy of what was there before. So whenever you change an era, change the reason for one, or change the stock weighting, raise the version. Two versions can then be compared, and a run made under the old assumptions can still be told apart from one made under the new."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the stock weighting","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--choose-the-stock-weighting","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":3,"where":"Stock weighting","text":"Stock weighting When CASS does not know exactly what type a building is, it blends the possible types using GEM's estimate of how common each is. This setting decides how “common” is measured. Replacement cost, the default, counts each type in proportion to what those buildings would cost to rebuild. Building count counts each building once, regardless of value. For insurance losses, replacement cost is usually the right choice, because it reflects where the money is."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose how design levels are decided","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--choose-how-design-levels-are-decided","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":4,"where":"How a design level is decided","text":"How a design level is decided GEM has a separate vulnerability function for each seismic design level: no design (CDN), low (CDL), moderate (CDM) and high (CDH). CASS needs a way to decide which levels apply to each property. There are two options: By a table of eras stated here. You list periods of years and the design levels buildings from each period might have. Choose this when your portfolios record the year each building was built, and you or a colleague know the history of the country's earthquake building codes. By the country's building stock alone. CASS uses GEM's estimate of how common each design level is across the whole country, whatever a building's age. Choose this when your portfolios do not record the year built, or nobody has yet researched the code history. You must write a short reason for this choice. The reason is saved, and is shown with every model version built on this set, so readers understand the limitation."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Fill in the eras, if you chose a table","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--fill-in-the-eras-if-you-chose-a-table","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":5,"where":"Design eras","text":"Design eras Under the Design eras heading, enter the eras from oldest to newest, one row each. The fields are numbered, so the first row is Era 1 up to year and so on. For each era, fill in: Up to year: the last year the era includes. Leave this empty for the final, most recent era, which runs up to today. Design levels: the levels a building from this period might have, separated by spaces, for example CDN CDL. Listing more than one means CASS will blend them. Reason: why you believe this, for example “Before the national seismic code was adopted”. Every era must have a reason. An era table (illustrative, not researched) Era 1: up to 1983, design levels CDN, reason: no national seismic code. Era 2: up to 2002, design levels CDN CDL, reason: first code, weakly enforced. Era 3: up to year left empty, design levels CDL CDM, reason: current code. A building from 1995 matches era 2, so it is modelled as a blend of no design and low design. CASS reads the table from the top and uses the first era a building's year fits. It will refuse a table that is out of order, that stops before the present day, that uses a design level GEM does not have, or that has an era without a reason. A property whose year built is not known uses the country's overall mix of design levels, whichever option you chose."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Record open questions, then build","anchor":"step-part-3-build-the-vulnerability-set--record-open-questions-then-build","card":"Part 3: Build the vulnerability set","step":6,"where":"Build the vulnerability set","text":"Build the vulnerability set Write any unresolved questions (one per line) and notes, then press the build button. A green message reports how many vulnerability functions and building classes were built. The set is saved as a draft. You may see a mention of channels. Some building classes include buildings that respond to different kinds of shaking: for example, when a building's height is unknown, the class includes both short buildings (which respond to quick, sharp shaking) and tall ones (which respond to slow, rolling shaking). CASS splits such a class into one part, or channel, for each kind of shaking, automatically. You do not need to do anything."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 4: Assemble the model version","anchor":"card-part-4-assemble-the-model-version","card":"","text":"This joins a grid and a vulnerability set into a model version. The hazard is added later, in part 6, because it takes longer to produce."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the grid and the vulnerability set","anchor":"step-part-4-assemble-the-model-version--choose-the-grid-and-the-vulnerability-set","card":"Part 4: Assemble the model version","step":1,"where":"Models → Build → Assemble a model version","text":"Models → Build → Assemble a model version Choose one of each from the lists. Both must be for the same country. CASS refuses a pair from two different countries, because the calculation would run without any error but the answer would be meaningless."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Give it a version and a label","anchor":"step-part-4-assemble-the-model-version--give-it-a-version-and-a-label","card":"Part 4: Assemble the model version","step":2,"where":"Model version and Label","text":"Model version and Label The model version has its own version number, separate from the grid's and the vulnerability set's. Give it a label people will recognise. You do not need to write down its limitations: CASS copies them from the grid and the vulnerability set automatically, so they cannot be forgotten."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Assemble it","anchor":"step-part-4-assemble-the-model-version--assemble-it","card":"Part 4: Assemble the model version","step":3,"where":"Assemble the model version","text":"Assemble the model version The new model version appears in the Model versions table further down the Build tab. It is a draft, and the Outstanding column lists what is still missing. At this point it will list the hazard, which is added next."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","anchor":"card-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model","card":"","text":"This part turns a published earthquake model into a hazard set: thousands of imagined earthquakes and the shaking each one causes in every cell. It happens on Models → Hazard, in three separate steps: upload, set up, and run. They are kept separate on purpose. A country-wide calculation can take hours, so CASS shows you what is in the model and exactly what the calculation will do before you start it, while mistakes are still cheap to fix. How long it takes, and how much it stores Measured on this installation for the Jakarta and Bandung region (962 cells, 20 paths, 10,000 imagined years): the whole run took about 9 minutes, and stored 233 MB of calculation results and a 38 MB footprint. CASS stores only shaking of 0.05 g or more, because no building in the world earthquake model is damaged by less; that keeps storage about a tenth of what it would otherwise be. A whole country takes far longer and stores more: the setup screen shows the most it could store before you save it. You do not need to worry about the computer's memory: the results are read a slice at a time, whatever their size."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Upload the hazard model","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--upload-the-hazard-model","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":1,"where":"Models → Hazard → Upload a model → Register model","text":"Models → Hazard → Upload a model → Register model Choose the hazard model's zip file and press Register model. CASS lists every file in it and records a fingerprint of each one (a code that changes if the file is ever altered), so it can always prove which files were used. It reads the settings file, and counts how many alternative scientific views the model contains (the paths through its logic tree; see Glossary ). It does not change anything inside the package."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the model and the grid","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--choose-the-model-and-the-grid","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":2,"where":"Registered models, then Area-peril grid","text":"Registered models, then Area-peril grid Select the model you uploaded from Registered models. Then choose the grid the shaking should be calculated on. Choose the same grid you used to assemble your model version in part 4; otherwise the hazard set will not fit it."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the region","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--choose-the-region","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":3,"where":"Region","text":"Region The region decides which cells are calculated. It affects the run time more than any other setting. The choices are: Jakarta and Bandung or Java and Madura: ready-made areas for Indonesia. Custom bounds: type the minimum and maximum latitude and longitude of your own rectangle. The whole grid: the entire country. This can take several hours. Only properties inside the region you calculate will receive a loss, so choose a region that covers your portfolio."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Review and adjust the settings","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--review-and-adjust-the-settings","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":4,"text":"The screen divides the model's settings into two kinds. The model's own science, such as its logic tree, is shown but cannot be changed: changing it would turn it into a different model while still carrying the original scientists' name. The calculation's settings can be changed. CASS starts from these values: Setting Starting value What it means, and why calculation_mode event_based Imagine individual earthquakes. A loss model needs them, and published models are usually set to a different mode (classical), so CASS always switches this. investigation_time 50 years The number of years in each batch of imagined earthquakes. ses_per_logic_tree_path 10 The number of batches for each path through the logic tree. Ten, so that rare losses rest on enough imagined years (see below). number_of_logic_tree_samples 20 The number of paths through the logic tree to use. Testing showed that a single path could be about 20% away from the scientists' overall view; combining 20 paths is much closer, and costs no more time. Together these give 50 × 10 × 20 = 10,000 imagined years. Why 10,000 and not 1,000. A 1-in-1,000-year loss is read from the worst imagined years. From 1,000 imagined years it is simply the single worst year, so a different set of imagined years could give a very different figure. From 10,000 imagined years it is the tenth-worst, which is much steadier. As a rule of thumb, divide the imagined years by the return period to see how many years a figure rests on: 10,000 ÷ 250 = 40 years for the 1-in-250-year loss. The cost. Both the calculation time and the storage grow with the imagined years. When you resolve the configuration (the next step), CASS shows the number of imagined years, how many years each return period rests on, and the most the hazard could store. If that is more than 100 GB, it warns you; you can then lower the number of batches or choose a smaller region."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Check the setup","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--check-the-setup","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":5,"where":"Resolve configuration","text":"Resolve configuration Press Resolve configuration. CASS shows every difference between the model as published and the calculation it will actually run, and lists any problems. Read these now: finding a problem here takes seconds, but finding it after several hours of calculation wastes the whole run. When you are happy, press Save this configuration."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Start the calculation","anchor":"step-part-5-upload-set-up-and-run-the-hazard-model--start-the-calculation","card":"Part 5: Upload, set up and run the hazard model","step":6,"where":"Saved configurations → Run","text":"Saved configurations → Run Open Saved configurations and press Run beside the one you saved. The calculation appears under Hazard and conversion runs on the Build tab, and in the Run monitor, where you can watch its progress. You can close the browser while it runs. When it finishes, the result is saved as a hazard set and appears in the Hazard sets table on the Build tab. CASS keeps the calculation's full results (its datastore) for 90 days and removes OpenQuake's own copy, so the calculation is only stored once."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Reusing a hazard set, and when it must be run again","anchor":"card-reusing-a-hazard-set-and-when-it-must-be-run-again","card":"","text":"A hazard calculation is slow, but it is done once for a grid, not once for each portfolio. After that, every portfolio and every model version on the same grid can use the same hazard set. A loss calculation then only looks up the shaking in the cells where the portfolio's properties are, which is why it takes seconds or minutes rather than hours. So settle the grid first Because any change to the grid means running the hazard again, finish the grid before starting a long hazard calculation on it."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"term","label":"You can reuse the hazard set when you change","anchor":"term-you-can-reuse-the-hazard-set-when-you-change","card":"Reusing a hazard set, and when it must be run again","text":"the portfolio, or bring in a new one; the vulnerability set or the assumption set, as long as its functions only need intensity measures the hazard set has; deductibles, limits, reinsurance or other financial terms; the model version, as long as it is on the same grid. The loss engine's package is rebuilt, which takes about a minute, but the hazard is not."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"term","label":"You must run the hazard again when you change","anchor":"term-you-must-run-the-hazard-again-when-you-change","card":"Reusing a hazard set, and when it must be run again","text":"the grid, in any way, including a new version of it: the cell numbers change, so the stored shaking would point at the wrong places; the hazard model, such as a new release of the earthquake model; the calculation settings: the imagined years, the number of paths, the random seed, or the region; the ground conditions joined to the cells; the intensity measures needed, if a new vulnerability set needs one the hazard set does not have."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"term","label":"In between: when CASS's intensity bands change","anchor":"term-in-between-when-cass-s-intensity-bands-change","card":"Reusing a hazard set, and when it must be run again","text":"The shaking is stored as bands of strength (intensity bins). If CASS's bands change, the stored shaking has to be sorted into the new bands, but the earthquakes themselves do not need calculating again. CASS does this from the calculation's stored results with Rebuild the footprint (part 6 below), as long as those results are still kept, which is 90 days after the calculation."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 6: Attach the hazard set to the model version","anchor":"card-part-6-attach-the-hazard-set-to-the-model-version","card":"","text":""},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Find the hazard set","anchor":"step-part-6-attach-the-hazard-set-to-the-model-version--find-the-hazard-set","card":"Part 6: Attach the hazard set to the model version","step":1,"where":"Models → Build → Hazard sets","text":"Models → Build → Hazard sets Each row is one hazard set. It shows the number of imagined earthquakes and years, the number of cells, and the intensity measures it contains. (Intensity measures are the different ways shaking strength is measured, such as PGA; each vulnerability function needs a particular one.)"},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Choose the model version and attach","anchor":"step-part-6-attach-the-hazard-set-to-the-model-version--choose-the-model-version-and-attach","card":"Part 6: Attach the hazard set to the model version","step":2,"where":"Attach","text":"Attach Choose the model version from the list in that row and press Attach. Only model versions built on the same grid and country are offered. CASS refuses to attach a hazard set that is missing an intensity measure the vulnerability functions need. Without it, some buildings would have no shaking and would show a loss of zero, which would look exactly like buildings that were not damaged. If this happens, run the hazard calculation again including the missing measure."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Rebuild a footprint, if a row asks you to","anchor":"step-part-6-attach-the-hazard-set-to-the-model-version--rebuild-a-footprint-if-a-row-asks-you-to","card":"Part 6: Attach the hazard set to the model version","step":3,"where":"Hazard sets → Rebuild the footprint","text":"Hazard sets → Rebuild the footprint A row may say Binned against intensity bins that have since changed, so it cannot be packaged . That happens when CASS's intensity bands are updated, as they were on 17 September 2026, when the strongest bands were widened because long calculations had shaking stronger than the old top band could hold. Press Rebuild the footprint. CASS sorts the stored shaking into the current bands, without running the earthquake calculation again, and follows it on the Run monitor. The result is a new hazard set with the same name and -b plus a short code at the end of its version, which says “Rebuilt from” the old one. Attach the new set to your model version. Once no model version uses the old set, its footprint is deleted to save space; its record stays so the history is complete. If the button is missing, the row says why: usually that the calculation's stored results are older than 90 days and have been removed, in which case the hazard has to be run again. A vulnerability set built before the same change also has to be built again from its GEM release (part 3): the package build refuses a vulnerability set and a hazard set that use different bands, because their numbers would be read against each other band by band."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 7: Publish the model version","anchor":"card-part-7-publish-the-model-version","card":"","text":"Publishing freezes the model version so its results can always be repeated, and makes it available to choose for runs. What to expect today A model version assembled on CASS at the moment will always show outstanding items, and so will always be published as research. Two of them cannot yet be cleared from the screens: the grid is still a draft (there is no screen for publishing a grid yet), and no end-to-end validation date has been recorded. This is expected, and matches what CASS is for: research. It does mean the Decision use run mode will not be available for these model versions."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Read what is still outstanding","anchor":"step-part-7-publish-the-model-version--read-what-is-still-outstanding","card":"Part 7: Publish the model version","step":1,"where":"Model versions → Outstanding","text":"Model versions → Outstanding This column lists everything that stops the model version counting as a finished model. One example is clipped hazard: shaking stronger than the highest band can record, which would understate the biggest losses."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Publish it","anchor":"step-part-7-publish-the-model-version--publish-it","card":"Part 7: Publish the model version","step":2,"where":"Publish, or Publish as research","text":"Publish, or Publish as research If nothing is outstanding, the button says Publish, and the model version becomes a finished model. If something is outstanding, the button says Publish as research. The model version can still be run, and its results are useful for research, but they cannot be approved, and the outstanding items remain listed for everyone to see."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"card","label":"Part 8: Get approval and build the Oasis package","anchor":"card-part-8-get-approval-and-build-the-oasis-package","card":"","text":"The loss engine, Oasis, cannot read a model version directly. It needs the model converted into its own files, called a package. Because the conversion involves scientific choices, a reviewer must approve it first. This takes two people. Oasis uses one package at a time Building a package for one model version replaces the package Oasis was using before. If someone then starts a run with a different model version, CASS stops it before it begins and names both model versions. This protects you from a serious mistake: without the check, the run would calculate with one model but label the results with another. To run an older model version again, build its package again first."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Ask for approval (modeller)","anchor":"step-part-8-get-approval-and-build-the-oasis-package--ask-for-approval-modeller","card":"Part 8: Get approval and build the Oasis package","step":1,"where":"Model versions → Request converter approval","text":"Model versions → Request converter approval Once a hazard set is attached, the model version's row shows Request converter approval. Press it. The row then says converter gate awaiting a reviewer."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Approve or reject (reviewer)","anchor":"step-part-8-get-approval-and-build-the-oasis-package--approve-or-reject-reviewer","card":"Part 8: Get approval and build the Oasis package","step":2,"where":"Models → Build → gates waiting on a decision","text":"Models → Build → gates waiting on a decision A reviewer opens the Build tab, where a card at the top lists the gates waiting on a decision. They read the request and press Approve or Reject. The reviewer must be a different person from the one who asked."},
  {"section":"model","sectionLabel":"Build a model","kind":"step","label":"Build the package (modeller)","anchor":"step-part-8-get-approval-and-build-the-oasis-package--build-the-package-modeller","card":"Part 8: Get approval and build the Oasis package","step":3,"where":"Build Oasis package","text":"Build Oasis package Once approved, the row shows Build Oasis package. Press it. CASS writes the shaking, the earthquake years, the vulnerability functions and the property-matching rules into the files Oasis reads. Use the Follow the conversion run link to watch it."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"card","label":"Before you start","anchor":"card-before-you-start","card":"","text":"A portfolio is your list of insured properties. Before CASS can use it, three things happen: it is brought in, it is checked and corrected, and it is published, which freezes it so any calculation made from it can be repeated exactly. Everything in this section happens in Exposure, which has three tabs: Portfolios, Import review and Financial structure. First, though, you need a project, because a portfolio belongs to one and the screens below stay empty without it. On the Portfolio dashboard, click a project's name to work in it, or press New project if there is none to click; anyone can create one, whatever their role. The project you are working in is named in the header at the top of every screen, and the Portfolios tab says so too when you have not chosen one."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"card","label":"Which route to use","anchor":"card-which-route-to-use","card":"","text":"There are two ways to bring a portfolio in. Which one depends on your data. Route A: your data is a spreadsheet in your own layout. This is the usual case. You copy the data into the CASS intake template, and CASS checks it thoroughly before it becomes a portfolio. Route B: your data is already in OED files. OED is the industry standard layout. If a colleague or another system has already produced OED files, you can upload them directly. Both are on the Portfolios tab, under Add a portfolio, headed Your data is a spreadsheet and Your data is already OED. That panel is what the tab shows until you pick a portfolio from the list; once you have picked one, press Add portfolio above the list to get it back."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"card","label":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","anchor":"card-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template","card":"","text":""},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Download the intake template","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--download-the-intake-template","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":1,"where":"Exposure → Portfolios → Add a portfolio → Your data is a spreadsheet → Download the intake template","text":"Exposure → Portfolios → Add a portfolio → Your data is a spreadsheet → Download the intake template Press Download the intake template to save the spreadsheet to your computer. Its columns are exactly the ones CASS reads, so filling it in avoids having to explain your own spreadsheet layout to CASS later."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Copy your data into the template","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--copy-your-data-into-the-template","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":2,"text":"Five columns decide whether a property can be modelled at all. Fill these in first, and exactly as described: a blank or unexpected value in any of them quietly removes the row, and later a whole customer, from the portfolio. Column What to put in it What happens if it is wrong or blank Latitude, Longitude The coordinates, as decimals. South and west are negative: Jakarta is −6.2088, 106.8456. Blank coordinates, or 0 and 0, make the row unclassified, and an unclassified row can never be promoted. Country The two-letter ISO code, such as ID. CASS checks the coordinates fall inside the country. It can only do that for Indonesia (ID) and Nepal (NP) today; a row in any other country is unclassified, however good its data. Geocode precision How exact the coordinates are. One of parcel, street or embedded (exact enough to trust the cell: cohort A), or locality, postcode or admin (approximate: cohort B). Blank, or any other word, makes the row unclassified. This is the single most common reason a promotion finds nothing to promote. Class of business Fire, spelled exactly like that, for property business. Promotion only takes Fire rows. Anything else, including fire in lower case, is left out. Liability and Engineering are excluded on purpose: liability has no building at the coordinates, and engineering risks need different treatment. Needs review yes for a row someone should check; leave it blank otherwise. A yes puts the row in cohort C, which keeps it out of a cohort A portfolio until you decide about it. The rest of the columns change the answer rather than gate it: Total insured value, and the four value columns ( Building value, Other structures value, Contents value, Business interruption value). Fill in the four where you know them. Where you give only a total, CASS splits it using the coverage split you choose when you promote. Primary site: marks the main location of a customer with several. It is only used if you choose the 70/30 allocation when promoting. Storeys: the number of floors. Fill it in wherever you know it. If you do not, leave it blank; 0 means the same thing. A building with no stated height is modelled as a blend of every height it could be, which is a less precise answer. (This column is called NumberOfStoreys in OED files, but Storeys in this template.) Year built: lets a table of design eras decide how well the building was designed for earthquakes. Without it, the country's overall mix is used. Occupancy and Construction: what the building is used for and what it is built from. They decide which vulnerability function answers the risk, so the more you fill in, the more specific the model can be. Perils covered: write QEQ, which is earthquake shaking. The template's own help says the same. Policy ID joins a property to its policy row, and Risk reference identifies the property itself. Two things this route fixes for you However you fill the template in, promotion records every peril as QEQ (earthquake shaking) and every value as USD. The Currency column is read but not used. So if your values are in another currency, say rupiah, the results will carry rupiah amounts labelled USD. Convert the values yourself before you upload, or treat the currency label on the results as wrong and say so when you share them. Route B, where you upload OED files, keeps the currency you state."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Upload the completed template","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--upload-the-completed-template","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":3,"where":"Completed template, As-at date, Import workbook","text":"Completed template, As-at date, Import workbook Under Completed template, choose your filled-in file. If the spreadsheet describes the portfolio at a date other than today (for example, the end of last quarter), enter that date as the As-at date. Then press Import workbook. The as-at date is a label, not an instruction. CASS records it with the import and shows it on the import review, so that two imports of the same book taken at different dates can be told apart. It changes nothing about what CASS reads, and no calculation uses it. Leaving it empty means the import is read as the position today. CASS reads the file exactly as it is and does not correct anything. A green message confirms it was read. At this point it is an import, not yet a portfolio: nothing can be run on it until you have reviewed and promoted it."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Open the import review","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--open-the-import-review","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":4,"where":"Exposure → Import review","text":"Exposure → Import review Choose your import from the Import list at the top. The page then shows these cards, in this order: What was read: the file, and what CASS made of it. Height, and what not knowing it costs: how many properties have no storey count, and what filling it in would change. Missing model inputs: which information the model needs is missing, and how much insured value sits behind each gap. What was included: the properties by country and class of business, and the cohort each one is in. Allocation and coordinate findings and Does the allocation assumption matter?: how value was shared between a customer's locations, and how far the answer would move under a different assumption. Do the coarse geocodes support their cells?: the check in step 6. What a result from this import may be used for: which kinds of run this data is good enough for. Review queue and Promote to a portfolio: steps 5, 7 and 8. Read it from the top. The point of this page is to show you the cost of each gap in the data before anybody relies on a result."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Work through the review queue","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--work-through-the-review-queue","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":5,"where":"Review queue → Record decision","text":"Review queue → Record decision Properties in cohort C have something that needs a person to check it, such as a location that looks wrong. They are listed in the review queue with the most valuable first, so your time goes where the money is. For each one, decide what should happen, write a short reason, and press Record decision. Your decision is recorded alongside the data; your original spreadsheet is never altered. You do not have to finish the whole queue. Any property still waiting for a decision is left out of the portfolio rather than modelled on a guess. Leaving one property out leaves its customer out CASS takes a customer's schedule whole or not at all: every one of an account's properties must qualify, or none of them are included. This is deliberate. A policy's total has to go somewhere, so including half a customer's sites would either pile their value onto the sites that were included, overstating them, or lose it altogether. But it does mean a single unresolved property removes that whole customer, so work the queue customer by customer, not just from the top."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Check whether the approximate locations can be trusted","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--check-whether-the-approximate-locations-can-be-trusted","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":6,"where":"Do the coarse geocodes support their cells?","text":"Do the coarse geocodes support their cells? Cohort B properties have coordinates that are only approximate, for example the centre of a postcode area rather than the building itself. CASS tests each one by trying positions across the whole area its geocode could mean, and checks whether it stays in the same cell. The report shows how much insured value is in a cell that cannot be relied on. If that value is large, it is worth improving those addresses before trusting the results."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Confirm the import has been checked","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--confirm-the-import-has-been-checked","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":7,"where":"Promote to a portfolio → Accept this import","text":"Promote to a portfolio → Accept this import At the bottom of the page, press Accept this import. This records that you have checked that what CASS read matches your spreadsheet."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Turn the import into a portfolio","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--turn-the-import-into-a-portfolio","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":8,"where":"Promote to an exposure version","text":"Promote to an exposure version Fill in the form that appears: Portfolio name: the name the portfolio will have. Cohort: which group of properties to include. The list does not say “A”, “B” or “C”. It names them Automated test cohort (cohort A, the precise geocodes), Geocoding-sensitivity cohort (B, the approximate ones) and Analyst-review backlog (C, the flagged ones). The hint under the field says how many properties the chosen group holds. Unclassified rows are not offered, and can never be promoted. Country: a model version covers one country, so a portfolio must too. By the same whole-schedule rule as above, a customer with sites in two countries is left out of both rather than split between them. Allocation: how a policy's total value is shared between its locations when the spreadsheet only gives the total. Equal across locations shares it evenly and is the normal starting point. Primary concentrated (70/30) puts 70% at the main location, and is useful as a test of how much the choice matters. Coverage split: how a single value is divided between the building, its contents and lost income, where the spreadsheet does not say. Occupancy: what to assume a building is used for, where the spreadsheet does not say. The last two are only used to fill gaps: anything your spreadsheet does state is never overwritten. Press Promote to an exposure version. This creates the portfolio as a draft."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Validate and publish the portfolio","anchor":"step-route-a-bring-in-a-spreadsheet-with-the-intake-template--validate-and-publish-the-portfolio","card":"Route A: Bring in a spreadsheet with the intake template","step":9,"text":"Follow the link in the green message, or go to Exposure → Portfolios and click the portfolio. Then continue from step 3 of Route B below."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"card","label":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","anchor":"card-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly","card":"","text":""},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Create a portfolio","anchor":"step-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly--create-a-portfolio","card":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","step":1,"where":"Exposure → Portfolios → Add a portfolio → Your data is already OED","text":"Exposure → Portfolios → Add a portfolio → Your data is already OED Type a Portfolio name and press Create version. This creates version 1 of the portfolio, as a draft, and opens it so you can attach its files."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Upload the files","anchor":"step-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly--upload-the-files","card":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","step":2,"where":"Source files","text":"Source files Upload the location file. If you have them, also upload the account file (which holds the policies) and the reinsurance files. CASS keeps the files exactly as you uploaded them."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Read the summaries","anchor":"step-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly--read-the-summaries","card":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","step":3,"text":"Several cards appear beneath the portfolio. The most useful to read first: Perspectives this data supports: which of the three losses (ground-up, insured, net of reinsurance) your files contain enough information for, and if not, what is missing. Value summary: the total insured value, split by coverage, by country and by currency. It matches your files exactly, so it is a good way to check nothing was lost."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Validate and fix problems","anchor":"step-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly--validate-and-fix-problems","card":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","step":4,"where":"Validate, then Validation findings and Portfolio rows","text":"Validate, then Validation findings and Portfolio rows Press Validate. CASS checks every row and lists the problems it finds under Validation findings. Each finding is either an error or a warning. Errors must be fixed before the portfolio can be published. Warnings do not stop you, but they are saved and shown alongside any result made from the portfolio, so they are worth reading. Use the filter at the top of the card to show only errors. To fix a row, find it in the Portfolio rows table at the bottom of the page and correct it there. Then press Validate again."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Publish","anchor":"step-route-b-upload-oed-files-directly--publish","card":"Route B: Upload OED files directly","step":5,"where":"Publish","text":"Publish Once nothing blocking remains, press Publish. The portfolio is now frozen and can be used in runs. A published portfolio can never be changed, so that every result made from it can be repeated. To correct it later, press Correct in a new version. That copies it into version 2 as a draft, which you can edit and publish in the same way."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"card","label":"Add the insurance and reinsurance details","anchor":"card-add-the-insurance-and-reinsurance-details","card":"","text":"A list of properties on its own only gives a ground-up loss: the cost of the damage. To see what the insurance pays (the insured loss) and what the insurer keeps after reinsurance, CASS needs the terms of the policies and the reinsurance treaties. This is called the financial structure. You can only add these to a draft portfolio. If the portfolio is already published, press Correct in a new version first."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Open the financial structure","anchor":"step-add-the-insurance-and-reinsurance-details--open-the-financial-structure","card":"Add the insurance and reinsurance details","step":1,"where":"Exposure → Financial structure","text":"Exposure → Financial structure This shows what the portfolio already contains: its accounts and their policy layers, any reinsurance contracts, and any problems found with them."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Add a policy for each account","anchor":"step-add-the-insurance-and-reinsurance-details--add-a-policy-for-each-account","card":"Add the insurance and reinsurance details","step":2,"where":"Add a policy","text":"Add a policy For each customer account, fill in: Account: choose the customer from the list. Policy reference: the policy's own reference number. Perils covered: already filled in with QEQ, which means earthquake shaking. Policy deductible: the amount the customer pays before the policy pays anything. Leave it empty if there is none. For each layer of cover: the attachment (the loss at which it starts paying; 0 for an ordinary policy), the limit (the most it pays; leave empty for no limit), and the signed share (the proportion the insurer has taken, as a decimal: 1 for all of it, 0.25 for 25%). One layer is filled in for you; press Add a layer if the policy has more than one."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Add each reinsurance treaty","anchor":"step-add-the-insurance-and-reinsurance-details--add-each-reinsurance-treaty","card":"Add the insurance and reinsurance details","step":3,"where":"Add a reinsurance contract","text":"Add a reinsurance contract Choose the Contract type, give it a Contract name, and fill in the perils it covers. The rest of the form changes to ask only for what that type needs: Type How it works What you fill in Quota share The reinsurer pays a fixed percentage of every loss. Ceded share (0.3 for 30%), and a Limit per event if there is one. Surplus share Works property by property, taking a share of each larger risk. The Risk level (per location, per policy or per account), a Limit per risk if any, and each risk it covers with its own ceded share. Catastrophe excess of loss Pays the part of each earthquake's total loss above a set amount, up to a limit. Attachment per event, Limit per event, and the Share of the layer ceded (leave empty for all of it). For every type, also fill in: Inuring priority: the order the treaties apply in. Priority 1 applies first; the treaty with priority 2 then only sees what is left. For example, a quota share is often applied before a catastrophe excess of loss. Placed share: the proportion of the treaty actually sold to reinsurers. Covers: The whole portfolio, or Named accounts or locations, in which case you list them."},
  {"section":"portfolio","sectionLabel":"Bring in a portfolio","kind":"step","label":"Check for problems, then publish","anchor":"step-add-the-insurance-and-reinsurance-details--check-for-problems-then-publish","card":"Add the insurance and reinsurance details","step":4,"where":"What is written","text":"What is written The What is written card shows the policies and contracts as CASS has saved them, and flags anything that would stop a run from working, so you find out now rather than partway through a calculation. To change a policy or contract, remove it and add it again. When there are no problems, go back to the Portfolios tab and publish the portfolio."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"card","label":"First, check the model fits your question","anchor":"card-first-check-the-model-fits-your-question","card":"","text":"Before setting up a run, open Models, which opens on Catalogue. Every analyst can see it, and it is the one screen that tells you what a model version can and cannot answer. Each model version is a card holding: Press Select on the card you want. The button then reads Selected, and that model version appears in the bar under the header, ready for the run."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"term","label":"Country, Peril, Version and Intensity measures","anchor":"term-country-peril-version-and-intensity-measures","card":"First, check the model fits your question","text":"What it covers. The country must match your portfolio: a model version covers one country only."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"term","label":"Publication state and Last validated","anchor":"term-publication-state-and-last-validated","card":"First, check the model fits your question","text":"Whether it is a finished model or a research prototype, and when it was last checked end to end."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"term","label":"Outstanding before this may be published as a full country model","anchor":"term-outstanding-before-this-may-be-published-as-a-full-country-model","card":"First, check the model fits your question","text":"Everything unfinished about it, listed rather than hidden. Read this before using its numbers for anything."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"term","label":"Exposure this version cannot model","anchor":"term-exposure-this-version-cannot-model","card":"First, check the model fits your question","text":"The kinds of building in your portfolio it has no vulnerability function for. If a lot of your value sits in those, the model is answering less of your portfolio than it appears to."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"term","label":"Peril scope statement","anchor":"term-peril-scope-statement","card":"First, check the model fits your question","text":"Which sub-perils of the earthquake are included and which are not, with the reason for each. CASS models shaking, so tsunami, fire following, liquefaction and landslide damage is not in the numbers. If a model version has no scope statement, the card says so in red: its results cannot honestly be called earthquake loss."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"card","label":"Set up and start a run","anchor":"card-set-up-and-start-a-run","card":"","text":"A run calculates the losses for one published portfolio using one model version. Everything is set up on one page, the Analysis builder, in the card What this run will use."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Check the project","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--check-the-project","card":"Set up and start a run","step":1,"where":"The header","text":"The header The run will belong to the project named in the header at the top of the screen. If it is the wrong project, choose the right one on the Portfolio dashboard first."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose the portfolio","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-the-portfolio","card":"Set up and start a run","step":2,"where":"Analysis builder → Portfolio","text":"Analysis builder → Portfolio Only published portfolios can be chosen. Drafts are shown greyed out, with a note to publish them first. This makes sure the data cannot change while the run is using it."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose the model version","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-the-model-version","card":"Set up and start a run","step":3,"where":"Model version","text":"Model version Choose the model for the country your portfolio is in. A model version marked as a research prototype (published with some work still unfinished) can be run, and its results are useful for research, but they cannot be approved."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose the assumption set, if there is a choice","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-the-assumption-set-if-there-is-a-choice","card":"Set up and start a run","step":4,"where":"Assumption set","text":"Assumption set An assumption set decides how CASS fills in unknown building details (construction, height and design level). Unless you have a reason to choose another, leave it on Baseline weights the model was built with . If the model version has no other assumption sets, this box cannot be changed. Changing the assumption set only changes the damage. Which cell each property is in, and its value, stay the same."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose what the run is for","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-what-the-run-is-for","card":"Set up and start a run","step":5,"where":"What this run is for","text":"What this run is for The choice decides how far the run goes and what its results may be used for: Choice When to use it Geometry only — map the book, calculate no loss To check which properties the model can place in a cell, before spending time on a full calculation. It calculates no loss. This is a good first run for any new portfolio. KRE-share technical loss An ordinary loss calculation on the share of each risk KRE holds. The results are for research. Portfolio-loss research A loss calculation for comparing alternatives, such as different assumption sets. The results are for research. Decision use A calculation whose result a reviewer may approve. It is only available with a finished model version (not a research prototype) and an approved assumption set; otherwise it shows “not yet available”."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose the perspective","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-the-perspective","card":"Set up and start a run","step":6,"where":"Financial perspective","text":"Financial perspective Choose which loss to calculate: ground-up, insured, or net of reinsurance. Ground-up needs only the locations. Insured also needs the policies. Net of reinsurance also needs the reinsurance contracts. The checklist in step 8 tells you if your portfolio does not have what the choice needs."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Choose the computing power, and name the run","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--choose-the-computing-power-and-name-the-run","card":"Set up and start a run","step":7,"where":"Resource profile and Run name","text":"Resource profile and Run name Each Resource profile shows how many processors and how much memory it gives, its time limit, and how many runs it has room for right now. The default is usually fine. Give the run a Run name you will recognise later, such as “Q3 portfolio, baseline”. If you leave it empty, the portfolio's name and version are used."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Check the checklist","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--check-the-checklist","card":"Set up and start a run","step":8,"where":"Before this run may be submitted","text":"Before this run may be submitted This card checks four things: a project is chosen; the portfolio is published; a model version is chosen; the portfolio has the information the chosen perspective needs. Anything not yet done has a link that takes you to the screen where you can do it."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Start the run","anchor":"step-set-up-and-start-a-run--start-the-run","card":"Set up and start a run","step":9,"where":"Submit analysis","text":"Submit analysis Press Submit analysis. The run is added to the queue and you are taken to the Run monitor. You can close your browser; the run carries on without it."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"card","label":"Watch the run","anchor":"card-watch-the-run","card":"","text":"The Run monitor shows how long the run has been going and, for the step it is on, how far through that step the engine reports it is. Neither of these is a prediction of when it will finish. A run goes through these steps in order. You do not need to do anything during most of them, but knowing what each does helps if a run stops. The first column is the name the screen shows; the short name in the second is what appears in a failure message and in the run's event log. Step, as the Run monitor names it Short name What happens Validate exposure validate_exposure The portfolio is checked again. Apply assumptions enrich Missing building details are filled in using the assumption set, and CASS records how much of the data is assumed rather than stated in your spreadsheet. Publish OED publish_oed The portfolio files are frozen. CASS checks that Oasis is serving this model version's package, and converts a portfolio in another currency at an approved rate. Run keys lookup keys Each property is matched to its cell, and to the vulnerability function for its type of building. Reconcile keys reconcile_keys CASS checks that the matched value, the value deliberately left out and the value it could not match add up to the portfolio's total. Value it could not match pauses the run (see below). Generate Oasis files generate_inputs The files Oasis needs are written. Validate Oasis files validate_inputs Those files are checked. Pre-loss smoke check smoke A quick trial with only a few of the largest earthquakes, to catch a broken setup: the smoke check. Calculate losses losses The full loss calculation. This is usually the longest step. Collect results collect The losses are gathered into results. Result review review The results are checked before they are shown."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"If the run pauses at the matching check","anchor":"step-watch-the-run--if-the-run-pauses-at-the-matching-check","card":"Watch the run","step":1,"where":"Keys reconciliation","text":"Keys reconciliation The Keys reconciliation card shows how much value was matched, how much was deliberately left out, how much could not be matched, and the portfolio's total. If some value could not be matched, the run pauses and shows Unmapped value is waiting to be accepted. This pause is on purpose: otherwise those properties would silently add nothing to the loss. To continue, write under Why the run should go on why the missing value is acceptable (at least a sentence), and press Ask for an exception. A reviewer who is not you then approves or rejects it. Once approved, press Resume the run. If you would rather fix the data, the When something goes wrong section explains the common causes."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Download files the run produced, if you need them","anchor":"step-watch-the-run--download-files-the-run-produced-if-you-need-them","card":"Watch the run","step":2,"where":"Evidence → Download","text":"Evidence → Download The Evidence card lists the files the run produced. Each download is recorded."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Try again, or stop a run","anchor":"step-watch-the-run--try-again-or-stop-a-run","card":"Watch the run","step":3,"where":"Retry and Cancel","text":"Retry and Cancel Cancel stops a run that is still going. Retry starts a failed run again as a new run. The history of the original run is kept unchanged, in Stage history, so you can always see what happened."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"card","label":"Read the results","anchor":"card-read-the-results","card":"","text":""},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Find the result","anchor":"step-read-the-results--find-the-result","card":"Read the results","step":1,"where":"Results","text":"Results Each perspective of each finished run appears as its own result. A badge beside its name shows its status: Research only (from a research prototype, so it cannot be approved), Awaiting review (could be approved but has not been), or Approved."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Read the main numbers","anchor":"step-read-the-results--read-the-main-numbers","card":"Read the results","step":2,"text":"At the top of each result: Average annual loss: the loss to expect in an average year. This is the most reliable number. Standard deviation: how much the yearly loss varies. Two return-period losses, for example the 100-year loss: a loss that large or larger has a 1% chance of happening in any one year. Below them is the exceedance probability (EP) curve, a chart of how likely each size of loss is. Open Full exceedance probability table to see the loss at every return period."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"See what is behind the numbers","anchor":"step-read-the-results--see-what-is-behind-the-numbers","card":"Read the results","step":3,"text":"Open these sections to understand where the loss comes from: Events behind this number: the imagined earthquakes that caused the most loss. Where the loss is: the average annual loss in each cell, showing which places contribute most. Scenario range: how much the answer changes under different assumption sets. What this number rests on: the model version, assumption set, run mode, valuation date, perspective, currency (with the rate, if the portfolio had to be converted) and approval status, then what is not included, the quality of the data, and Where the uncertainty comes from."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Compare two runs","anchor":"step-read-the-results--compare-two-runs","card":"Read the results","step":4,"where":"Compare two runs","text":"Compare two runs Choose a starting result (the baseline) and a result to compare with it. CASS shows how the numbers differ and lists what changed between the two runs that could explain it, such as a different model version or assumption set."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"step","label":"Export or approve","anchor":"step-read-the-results--export-or-approve","card":"Read the results","step":5,"where":"Export package and Approve for decision use","text":"Export package and Approve for decision use Export package downloads the numbers together with a record of exactly how they were produced, so someone else can trace them. A reviewer can press Approve for decision use on a result from a finished model version. An approved result is frozen; running again creates a new result rather than changing it."},
  {"section":"run","sectionLabel":"Run and read results","kind":"card","label":"Reading the numbers with care","anchor":"card-reading-the-numbers-with-care","card":"","text":"Four things are easy to miss and change how much weight a number can bear. CASS reads about 10% lower than the engine it is checked against. The one book measured both ways — the 64-location Jakarta–Bandung portfolio — gave an average annual loss of 566,728 through CASS against 626,732 calculated directly in OpenQuake from the same ground shaking: a ratio of 0.904. At individual return periods the two ranged from 0.70 to 1.26 of each other. Nobody has yet agreed how close is close enough, so this is recorded rather than passed or failed. Treat a CASS loss as an estimate with roughly that much room around it, not as a figure accurate to the last pound. Trust the average more than the extremes. A 1-in-1,000-year loss is read from the worst imagined years: from the default 10,000 imagined years it is the tenth-worst, and from a hazard set made with only 1,000 years it is the single worst, so a different set of imagined years could give quite different figures. The screen that sets up a hazard calculation shows how many years each return period rests on. The average annual loss uses every year, so it is much steadier. Ground with no measurement is treated as rock. Where the hazard model has no soil measurement within 15 km of a cell, CASS assumes rock. Rock shakes less than soft ground, so this can make losses too low on soft ground. Across Indonesia, about 79% of cells have no nearby measurement. The hazard run's site report shows how much of the grid this affected. An approximate address gives an approximate cell. A property located only to its town or postcode could really be in a neighbouring cell with different shaking. The geocoding sensitivity report shows how much value this affects. “Net of reinsurance” is what the insurer keeps. The amount the reinsurers pay is the insured loss minus this figure."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Who this section is for","anchor":"card-who-this-section-is-for","card":"","text":"Administration is the screen that describes the installation itself: which versions of everything are running, whether the calculation engines are reachable, who has which role, and a record of every governed action anyone has taken. Everyone can open it and read most of it. That is deliberate: if you are asking a colleague or a supplier for help, the facts they will ask for are on this screen, and you should not need an administrator to read them out. Only a platform administrator can change anything, and a message at the top says so when you cannot."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Engine health","anchor":"card-engine-health","card":"","text":"The first card, and the one to check when something is not working. CASS does the calculation through two separate engines, OpenQuake for the earthquakes and Oasis for the losses, and this card asks each one whether it is alive and reports what came back. A red message naming an engine means runs that need it will fail until it is back. That is an administrator's problem to fix, not yours, but seeing it here saves you investigating a run that was never going to work. CASS asks the engines through its own API, so what you see is what the platform can reach, not what your browser can."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Exposure data standards","anchor":"card-exposure-data-standards","card":"","text":"Which version of OED (the industry exposure format) CASS checks your portfolios against, and any other versions registered beside it. Worth quoting if someone sends you files from another system and the import complains about a column."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"This installation","anchor":"card-this-installation","card":"","text":"The identifying facts about this copy of CASS. Quote them in full when you report a problem."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"term","label":"API version","anchor":"term-api-version","card":"This installation","text":"The version of the CASS platform itself."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"term","label":"OED schema","anchor":"term-oed-schema","card":"This installation","text":"The exposure format version portfolios are checked against."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"term","label":"Artifact backend","anchor":"term-artifact-backend","card":"This installation","text":"Where the large files runs produce are stored."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"term","label":"Default resource profile","anchor":"term-default-resource-profile","card":"This installation","text":"The computing power a run is given when nobody chooses otherwise."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"term","label":"Use of model data","anchor":"term-use-of-model-data","card":"This installation","text":"A standing statement that the models and data CASS carries are used inside Klapton Re only: not sold and not passed outside the company."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Tested engine combinations","anchor":"card-tested-engine-combinations","card":"","text":"The combinations of engine versions this installation has actually been tested against. It matters because a model package built under one combination is not guaranteed to behave the same under another, so CASS records which ones have passed their checks rather than assuming any version will do."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Execution profiles, and queues, storage and retention","anchor":"card-execution-profiles-and-queues-storage-and-retention","card":"","text":"An execution profile (called Resource profile when you set up a run) is a named allowance of computing power: so many processors, so much memory, a time limit, and a limit on how many runs may use it at once. They exist so one very large job cannot take over the machine and stop everyone else working. Queues, storage and retention, which only an administrator sees, shows what each profile is running now, how much the file store holds, and what the housekeeping sweep will delete next. If your run is queued rather than running, this is where you find out what it is waiting behind."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Users and roles","anchor":"card-users-and-roles","card":"","text":"Everyone with an account, and what each may do. The table has a row per person and these columns: Column What it means Person Their name and username. Platform role Portfolio analyst, Underwriter, Catastrophe modeller, Reviewer or Platform administrator. See Start here for what each can do. May publish models Whether they can build and publish model versions: modellers and administrators. May decide gates Whether they can approve or reject work at a gate: reviewers and administrators. Nobody can approve their own request, whatever this says. Local install Whether they are allowed to run an approved copy of CASS on their own machine. Access Shown to administrators only: where a role is changed. A platform role decides what somebody may do anywhere in CASS. It does not decide which projects they can see: that is project membership, granted separately per project."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"Audit trail","anchor":"card-audit-trail","card":"","text":"An append-only record of every governed action: who did it, when, and what it touched. Nothing in it can be edited or deleted, which is what makes it worth having. Use it to answer “who approved this?” or “what changed between these two runs?”. You can filter it, including by the correlation ID that a run carries, which ties together everything that run did. If a filtered search comes back empty An empty trail under a correlation ID usually means the ID came from somewhere other than a run. Copy it from the run in the Run monitor rather than from a log message."},
  {"section":"administration","sectionLabel":"Administration","kind":"card","label":"What to send when you ask for help","anchor":"card-what-to-send-when-you-ask-for-help","card":"","text":"What you were trying to do, and the exact message on screen. The address in your browser's address bar. From This installation: the API version and the OED schema. For a run that failed: its correlation ID, and the step it stopped on, from the Run monitor. Whether Engine health shows anything unreachable."},
  {"section":"problems","sectionLabel":"When something goes wrong","kind":"card","label":"Common problems, why they happen, and what to do","anchor":"card-common-problems-why-they-happen-and-what-to-do","card":"","text":"When CASS refuses to do something, it is almost always protecting a result from being quietly wrong. The message on screen says why. This table covers the most common cases. What you see Why it happens What to do There is no Hazard or Build tab under Models These tabs are only shown to modellers and administrators. If you need them, ask an administrator to change your role. Screens are empty, or keep asking for a project No project has been chosen. Go to the Portfolio dashboard and click a project's name. There are no projects to choose from Nobody has created one yet, or you have not been made a member of any that exist. A new installation starts with none. Press New project on the Portfolio dashboard. Anyone can create one, whatever their role, and you own the one you create. If you believe a project already exists, ask its owner to add you. The grid cell counter shows a yellow warning The specification would make more cells than the limit, or a refinement's cells are not smaller than the base cells. Make the base resolution larger, make the tiles or refinements smaller, or give the refinement a smaller cell size. The warning says which problem it is. CASS refuses to build a vulnerability set No GEM release has been chosen. Go to Models → Build → GEM release and press Use this release. A country is greyed out in the vulnerability country list GEM describes that country's buildings in a classification (HAZUS) that CASS cannot translate yet. It cannot be built at the moment. The reason is shown beside the country. CASS refuses to assemble a model version The grid and the vulnerability set are for different countries. Choose a grid and a vulnerability set for the same country. CASS refuses to attach a hazard set The hazard set does not include an intensity measure the vulnerability functions need, so some buildings would get no shaking. Run the hazard calculation again, including the missing measure. There is no Build Oasis package button Either no hazard set is attached, or the converter approval has not been granted yet. Attach a hazard set, press Request converter approval, and ask a reviewer to approve it. Promoting says no business has its whole schedule in cohort A Fire Nothing qualified. Usually the Geocode precision column is blank or holds a word CASS does not recognise, or Class of business is not exactly Fire, or the country is one CASS has no coordinate check for (only Indonesia and Nepal today). It can also be one unresolved property taking its whole account out with it. Open What was included on the import review to see which cohort the rows landed in, fix the columns in the template, and import it again. Bring in a portfolio, Route A, step 2 lists the exact values each column takes. A customer you expected is missing from the portfolio CASS takes a customer's schedule whole or not at all, so one property in another cohort, another country, or another class of business removes all of that customer's sites. Find that customer's properties in the review queue and resolve every one of them, or promote the cohort that holds them all. Results say USD but the values were in another currency A portfolio brought in through the intake template records every value as USD and every peril as earthquake shaking, whatever the template said. Convert the values before uploading, or upload OED files instead (Route B), which keep the currency you state. A portfolio will not publish There are still blocking problems in the data. Fix them: see Validation findings on the Portfolios tab, and the findings on the Financial structure tab. The Analysis builder will not let you submit One of the four checklist items is not done. Follow the link beside the item that is not done. A run stops at publish_oed and names two model versions Oasis is using a different model version's package, probably because someone built another package since. Build this model version's package again (Build a model, part 8), then retry. A run stops at publish_oed and mentions a currency The portfolio is in a currency for which there is no approved exchange rate. Ask your administrator about getting an exchange rate approved for that currency. A run pauses at reconcile_keys Some of the portfolio's value could not be matched to a cell or a vulnerability function. Either fix the data and run again, or ask for an exception with a reason and resume once a reviewer approves. Properties are reported as outside the domain, meaning outside the area the grid covers (the technical label is fail_ap) They fall outside every tile of the grid, or outside the region the hazard was calculated for. Wrong coordinates, such as latitude and longitude swapped or a missing minus sign, are a common cause. Check their coordinates first. If they are correct, the grid needs another tile (in a new grid version), or the hazard needs calculating over a larger region. Properties fail with fail_v No vulnerability function matches the building details given. Check the construction, occupancy and number of storeys for those properties in the import review. There is no Approve for decision use button The result came from a research prototype, or your role is not reviewer or administrator. An approvable result needs a finished model version and a reviewer."},
  {"section":"problems","sectionLabel":"When something goes wrong","kind":"card","label":"Finding out more","anchor":"card-finding-out-more","card":"","text":"Every run keeps its full history on the Run monitor. It also has a reference code (a correlation ID) that appears in the engines' technical logs, which helps an administrator investigate a problem. If you are asking someone for help, send them the address of the screen you are on and the exact message shown. To point them at a section of this guide, send the address from your browser. For people who drive CASS from code: the API and its documentation are at /api/docs/. For administrators: how to install and run CASS is in platform/docs/running-cass.md, and the reasons behind the platform's design are in platform/docs/adr/."},
];
